window.HELLSCRIPT_WIKI_HISTORY=window.HELLSCRIPT_WIKI_HISTORY||{};window.HELLSCRIPT_WIKI_HISTORY["casual-ui-fidelity-audit-20261020.en"]=[{"body": "# Casual UI mockup fidelity audit — 2026-10-20\n\n[한국어](Casual_UI_Fidelity_Audit_20261020.md)\n\n## Method\n\nEvery board of a mockup canvas (Claude Design) is rendered on a local server and every text, box and image rect is extracted in board pixels (`tools/ui_fidelity/measure.html`). The same values are extracted from the real window at the same size (a floating Unity Game View resized to 440×956 / 956×440 / 1600×900, `UiFidelityDump`) and compared with `compare.py` (text matched by string, boxes by IoU). Boards: portrait 440×956, phone landscape 956×440, PC 1600×900.\n\n## Shared cause and shared fix\n\n- Earlier windows used game units (`UiTheme.Scale`) with ad-hoc constants that followed the mockup \"in proportion\" only: on the same board text was about 2 px small and cells, tabs and gaps differed.\n- `UiMock` (board class + `UiTheme.Scale / reference scale`) makes one canvas unit equal one mockup pixel on the reference board and scales the board proportionally elsewhere. A window uses the mockup CSS tokens (`--cell`, `--head`, `--fs` …) directly.\n- `StorageSurface.shadowOutside` and an extended button chrome hang the box-shadow outside the box, so the layout rect equals the mockup border-box.\n- `EquipmentSlotView.Paint(…, CellRecipe)` draws the inner slot layout (art 86% at 7%/5%, level .17, chips .14/.2/.22) proportionally; other windows keep their look without a recipe.\n\n## Warehouse (applied)\n\nMeasured against the old window (portrait): title 22.8→25, tab numerals 8.7→11, category 10.9→13, footer currency 11.9→16 px, cell 56.5→52, vault tab 38→46, plus category row, well, rail and footer positions now follow the mockup. Final measurement: normal and bulk states on all three boards are within 0.5 px in font size (integer font rounding) and mostly within 2 px in box position and size; only the PC tool chips and the usage pill are off by up to about 5–9 px. Dialogs match in width and font size; the expand dialog matches the 382 px frame height, help and open-tab are within ±5 px. The grade popover matches in position and was +14 px tall; that was corrected but not re-measured.\n\nKnown remaining gaps: drag ghost, pause pill and toast boards are not measured yet. Detail and preset dialog internals belong to shared parts (`ItemDetailView`, `CharacterEquipmentView`). The brand letter spacing is drawn by `UiTextSpacing` but the dump reads glyph width before the effect; the usage pill width differs from the mockup sample (\"12 / 60\") with the count string.\n\n## Rune master (applied)\n\n`RuneMasterWindow.Layout.cs` draws the reshape and ascend tabs in the mockup's CSS pixels (per-board head, footer, chips, slots, material cells and the fuse button). Final measurement: the three reshape boards and the PC idle/pair/error states differ in only 1–2 of 12–13 texts (the brand letter-spacing width and a 4% narrower long note on the landscape board), and portrait box deviation is zero. Real differences fixed in this pass: footer skip-button height (landscape +4, portrait +2), landscape ascend group row height 51→53, the ascend summary line height now uses the mockup's fractional size instead of the rounded font, two-digit grade chip counts wrapping on portrait, and the initial scrollbar position. Remaining: the bottom empty bag cells on PC/landscape look shorter in the dump only because the viewport clips them (raw height 91.6 matches); the usage pill differs by about 3 px with digit width; the ascend board is driven with a different group state than each mockup board (each board registers a different cell count), so the registered-materials row is matched by formula (material and result cell heights) only.\n\n## Shared confirm and toast (applied)\n\n`CasualNoticeLayout` now computes the mockup's CSS pixels times `UiMock.CanvasScale` in physical pixels (`For(safe, scale)`, the device value is `Current()`); before, it used raw `Screen` pixels and drew at about a third of the mockup on a DPR 3 phone. The card is border-box like the mockup (the border sits outside the padding); the toast groups portrait with landscape for border, shadow, padding and the 9 px gap (`.o-land .tst,.o-port .tst`), the info badge and text are one centred group, and the badge is drawn as the mockup's white disc, navy ring and blue \"i\" (`CasualNoticeInfo`) instead of the old sprite. Measured on three boards: font size difference 0, text position within 3 px, toast box size equal. The toast's vertical place follows the live HUD top and was not measured. At the 1:1 editor board text is about 4% narrower because legacy `Text` rounds glyph advances to whole pixels; at device scale that falls below 1%.\n\n## Town plaza (partly applied)\n\nThe existing build already matched font sizes and most box positions. Fixed in this pass: the interaction card detail now starts on its first line (+10 px), the talk button carries the mockup's chat glyph as a centred row, the title plate's 3 px shadow is part of its rect, and the PC guide panel uses the 3 px border inset and 556 px height. Kept on purpose: the top-right cog, content dock and event button follow PR #144 (2026-10-18, \"shortcuts beneath a painted cog\") and the `menu-*` medallion art, so they differ from the mockup's flat cog and dock place. The shared HUD text sizes and the portrait slot row were matched under \"Shared HUD\" below. The place name contains a glyph outside the Jua subset (\"잿\"), so it falls back to Noto and is about 9 px wider. The card and name tags are enlarged by a transform scale (cardScale/scale), so on small canvases (0.54 on portrait) their text is rasterised at canvas resolution and magnified (may look soft, follow-up).\n\n## Daily quests and attendance (applied)\n\nThe three pages are redrawn on `EventsPlate` (the mockup's cream plate with masthead, three tabs and footer strip) and `ContentWindowView`'s fitted mode (`Open(..., fit)`, `RepaintFitted` draws only the scrim and one plate). Every size is the mockup's CSS pixels; `UiMock.WindowScale` (scale from the whole screen) and `UiMock.Plate` (safe area less 8 px, PC capped at 1500×810) place the plate. The old build stretched with the screen aspect. Final measurement: daily quests have 32/37 (portrait) and 37/43 (PC) texts at the mockup's size and place (the rest are ≤4% width differences and glyphs outside the Jua subset), 7-day attendance 21/21 on PC and 28-day 17/17. The tab waiting dots, footer strips, remaining time (HH:MM:SS) and claim toast follow the mockup. Because of the mockup's `.dq button{margin:0;font:inherit;color:inherit}`, the DOM draws tab and claim text as navy Noto 14 px and the title as gilt Jua, so the DOM dump was the reference. Remaining: the dump cannot read the shadow (UiWindowFrame +5 px) or clipped scroll content, and the daily claim/refresh toast and the closed states were not measured.\n\n## Shared HUD (applied)\n\n`GlobalHudLayout.HudFont` sets the level, HP, resource, EXP, caption and cooldown text to the mockup's per-board size (PC, landscape, portrait), and the portrait slot row baseline (147) and shortcut captions (below the slots) follow the mockup. Checked on plaza HUD captures: Lv 17/13/13, HP and resource 13/10/10, EXP and captions 15/11/11, cooldown numbers 26/15/17 (PC/portrait/landscape). The plaza and battle HUD mockups disagree by about 1 px on HUD text and on the portrait slot size (46 vs 42); the game follows the plaza mockup.\n\n## Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)\n\nThe legacy `HuntEdictWindow` is about to be removed, so work stopped there on the user's instruction. Applied: the mockup's border-box frame (padding inside the 3 px border, header, rule and tab spacing, a dashed footer rule with the buttons), drop shadows hanging outside the rect so control sizes equal the mockup boxes (`UiButtonFace.ShadowOutside`, `HuntEdictSurface.shadowOutside`), the header subtitle pill, tab icon and label placement, the overview switch row and style cards, the group editor (search row, group chips, white section card, quick-preset stack, option rows), the skill manage list, the equipped-skills dock, the policy page header, preset tabs and the rail dock. Measured: on PC the combat, loot, bag, repeat and explore (custom rows) tabs match almost every text in size and place, with 1-2 box deviations. What remains is the Noto fallback for strings with the middle dot (not in the Jua subset, 3 to 14 px wider) and `.5` font sizes rounding. Not done: presets, recommended equipment, dialogs and the survival popups (hazard canvas) - skipped because they are legacy. Found on the way: the window's palette fields read `Resources` in a `MonoBehaviour` field initializer, which made the first text transparent; they are now read on use.\n\n## Settings window (applied)\n\nThe settings canvas used `ApplyScaler(..., *.5)`, which drew it about 20% smaller than the mockup on phones; it now uses `UiMock.CanvasScale` (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel) and a per-board table `SettingsLook` (padding, gap, font, tab height, chip, button, bar, seal) for the frame (title, close, rule, tab row, scrolling body), card padding, section headers, body and muted copy (with line height, `SettingsParagraph`), notices, sliders (bar `barh`, 17 px marker, ticks), choice cards (map, language, hero), buttons and the UID strip. The 12 px body scrollbar takes width only when content overflows; the audio tab uses volume rows (no gap) and a mute card (checkbox and two lines); the character tab holds the hero cards in one card. Measured: PC screen tab 17 of 19 texts and language 11 of 12 match; audio, character and account have the structure and 7-9 texts at the same size and place. Remaining: the value `%` drawn apart from the number, the audio bar markers, account UID and pending counts (demo data), notice line counts (text width); the guide and combat-state tabs use the same table but were not measured separately. `CasualSettingsTests` now measure in mockup units (tab width and height, UID strip, slider touch area).\n\n## Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)\n\nThe mockup (`Equipment shop - casual`) differs from the old screen in structure, so the window was redrawn in the mockup's pixels instead of retuning values (`EquipmentShopWindow.Mock*.cs`; the gamble merchant and the auto-selection settings shared with salvage keep their old layout). One canvas unit is one mockup pixel and the PC board is 1440 wide, hence the new `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)`. Built new: the header (brand, place, title, close), the tab row with the restock countdown, rarity and order selects (small list popups) and the category strip, 56 px offer rows with the \"Small upgrade\" tag, the inventory grid (8 columns, 50 slots, categories, the multi-select bar, the auto-select notice band), two comparison popups for buy, sell and buyback (deltas, lost properties, upgrade record, fact table) and the dock under them, the footer (gold, gems, status, History, Help), thin scrollbars that take width only when content overflows, the dialog frame (sale confirmation, item details, presets, history, help) and the auto-selection settings (rarity chips, equipment kinds, details, exclusion rules). History is backed by `EquipmentShopState.ledger` (the last 30 purchases, sales and buybacks, newest first; an additive save field, old saves load an empty list). At the user's request the item picture in the comparison popups is larger than the mockup's 40 px (72 px on PC, 52 px on phones). Measured: on the PC buy, sell, multi-sale, auto-select settings and details boards texts and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px and control sizes match (grid cell 63 px, offer row 56 px, popup 425.5 px). Remaining: the middle dot missing from the Jua subset makes the title and place strings fall back to Noto (3 to 14 px wider) and Jua digits are about 10% wider, no \"Unique\" rarity chip (a reserved legacy bit that cannot be selected), the mockup's EN/KO toggle is left to the language setting, and the enlarged item picture (requested). 60 new strings carry English in `en.txt`.\n\n## Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)\n\nThe blacksmith mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Blacksmith - casual`) differs from the old screen in structure (free-floating round header buttons, five pill tabs, rounded panel cards, a docked action bar), so it was redrawn in the mockup's unit `U` (PC 14 px, phone landscape 12.32 px, phone portrait 13.86 px). Built new: the shared frame (header, tabs, wallets, lock tip, sealed screen, toast), option change (item list, option rows, selection dock), slot enhancement (slot rows, work status, workshop, growth table, unlock popup), gear enhancement (item list, growth steps, quote dock, auto-enhance settings and result, confirm popup), core crafting (core equipment panel, catalogue, forge, quality gauge, result and history popups), the item picker sheet and the help dialog. Files are split into `BlacksmithWindow.*Screen.cs`, `*Dialogs.cs`, `Modal.cs`, `Overlays.cs` and `CoresEquip.cs`; the capture driver is `UiFidelityForge.cs` (specs enchant/slots/gear/cores with `+confirm`, `+hit`, `+complete`, `+workshop`, `+unlock`, `+plan`, `+core-*`, `sealed`, `locktip`). Measured: on the PC, phone landscape and phone portrait boards text sizes match and text and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px; only a few knife-edge line breaks differ because Jua is about 10% wider. The mockup's demo-only history and reset popups were not built (the game has no reroll history). New strings carry English in `en.txt` and the old ones were pruned.\n\n## Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)\n\nThe jeweler mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Jeweler - casual`) differs from the old window (three tabs, gem list, potion rows), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel, `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)`). Its CSS answers the board size with container queries, so `JewelerLook` starts from the base rules and overrides them in the stylesheet's own order (wide >=1550, mid 681-1100, short <=600, mobile <=680). Built new: the masthead (gem emblem, gilt title, active elixir, tier values, sockets, close), the service bar, the 6x6 owned-gem matrix with the inspected gem and tier conversion (1, 5, all), six gem tabs and six elixir cards (three effects, owned, state), the docked brew bar (quantity minus/plus/max input and the brew button), the footer note, the toast and the tier values dialog. Game-side changes: `Jeweler.Craft(count)` and `CraftLimit` let the player brew any quantity (the `GemBatch` versions are unchanged); the elixir pictures use the game's approved elixir art in the same box instead of the mockup's SVG vials. The mockup's demo-only parts (the \"try elixir\" button, the reset dialog, the draft note) were not built; the close button, the sockets button and the broken-gem purchase row were added because the game needs them. `MockKit` is the new shared pixel helper (text boxes, cards, press, icons, thin scrolling) the next windows reuse. Measured (`UiFidelityJeweler.cs`: brew, convert, empty, crafted, balance, with `@inv|@pot|@body|@potions` scroll variants): on PC 1440x810 and 1440x900, phone landscape and phone portrait every text size matches and positions sit within 0-2 px (brew dock, matrix, cards and dialog included). Remaining: the description paragraph sits 1-2 px low, dashed outlines of empty cells, the game's elixir art instead of the SVG vials, wrap widths of the shield and long sentences (Unity breaks Hangul lines differently); the wide (>=1550) and short (601-760 tall) rules were transcribed from the CSS but could not be measured. New strings carry the mockup's English in `en.txt` (\"Jeweler (casual)\") and 21 old entries were pruned.\n\n## Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)\n\nThe rift entry mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Rift Entry - casual`) differs from the old window (title row, scrolling body, bottom buttons), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels. `RiftEntryWindow.Fit` uses `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)` to make one canvas unit one mockup pixel (the boards are full-bleed, no safe area) and takes the mockup's `--b` (PC/landscape `clamp(9, min(1.4583%W, 2.9167%H), 24)`, portrait `min(3.5%W, 1.842%H)`) as the one unit every size is a multiple of. Wide boards use three columns (art .95/3 · fatigue, potions, skills · entry), portrait stacks them and shares the spare height as `(H-content)/4` gaps (at least .3B). New: the masthead (back, logo, divider, title, abyssal coins, close), the portal art panel with tier picker, record and first-clear chest, the fatigue gauge (daily and restored spans, reset time, carry-over), three equipped-potion rows, five skill tiles (four actives and the ultimate), the services row, three entry buttons (1x, 1.5x, resume) and the help button. Modals follow the mockup's `.modal` rules (wide, narrow <=650, short <=570) through `ModalLook` and are drawn into their own fixed views: 1.5x confirm, fatigue recovery confirm, fatigue help, potion picker (six elixir tabs plus an ordinary-potion tab, six tier cards), skill/ultimate picker (three-column option cards, details box, Close/Replace) and first-clear rewards (virtualized 1,000-tier list). Game rules and saves are unchanged (`Store.ReplaceRiftSkill`, `game.SelectRiftPotion` and the fatigue paths are the same) and the window keeps its old control names (`rift-enter-fast`, `rift-skill-N`, `rift-potion-N`, ...). The two portal paintings (tall and wide) were generated with Codex and installed under `Resources/Art/RiftEntry/Casual/` (record: `Docs/Art/RiftEntryPortal20261020/`, model not reported, the generator's own check flagged the composition, so not production-approved). Measurement (`UiFidelityRift.cs`: lobby, newtier, claimed, empty, mixed, limit, poor and the modals fast, recover, help, potion, potiondiamond, skill, ultimate, rewards): the three lobby boards and the confirm, recover and help modals match in text size with positions inside 0-1 px; the potion, skill, ultimate and rewards modals are within +-0.5 px in text size (legacy Text uses whole sizes) and 0-2 px in position. Deliberate differences from the mockup: the potion tabs gain an \"Ordinary potion\" tab the mockup lacks (ordinary potions stay equippable), the reward rows show the real reward chest art with claim state and unlock lines instead of the empty placeholder and the \"next content\" hint moved from the lobby to the top of that modal, and the skill details append rank, damage, cost and activation order (real data) under the mockup's one-line description, so the body scrolls on short boards. Titles containing a middle dot (.) are drawn in Noto because of the Jua glyph limit. 38 new strings were added to `en.txt` in the mockup's English and four changed ones (Replace skill, Enter Rift, Change potion, the tagline) were aligned to it. The native smokes (`RuntimeRiftEntrySmoke*` and friends) were adapted to the new structure (fixed view, modal names, virtual list) but not run this time.\n\n## Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)\n\nThe gear offer mockup (`Rift gear offer UI - casual`: battle cards, details, compare and settings) differs from the old windows (a header/tabs/scroll shell hosting the shared `ItemDetailView` and `EquipmentComparisonView`), so the detail/compare and settings windows were redrawn in mockup pixels. `RiftRecommendationWindow` uses `RiftEntryWindow.Fit` (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel; cards, rows and cells live in `RiftRecommendationWindow.Look.cs`) and draws the compare tab, the ring target choice (left/right), the worn and recommended cards, the score line (old -> new), the real stat rows (base, fixed resistance, affixes, special effects, set bonuses) and the pinned Equip/Close row. `RiftGearSettingsWindow` draws the numbered sections (I-IV), pill choices, per-position detail panels (threshold fields, add-option list), the time slider and the pinned Save/Close row as in the mockup; the landscape board is a 560x424 window whose body alone scrolls (the mockup shows it scrolled by 26 px, so it matches at the same offset). The battle cards (`GameUI.GearRecommendations`) follow the mockup card rules (inner 1 px border, radius 12, shadow outside): the card is 92x150, the score pill 80x17, the Equip button 58x20 and the gear/close buttons 24 px, all at the mockup positions. Game rules and transactions (`EquipInventoryItem`, `SaveRiftGearOfferSettings`) and the old control names are unchanged, and the real item and setting values replace the mockup's samples in the same row look (a longer real stat list makes the body scroll). Decorations only the mockup has (the Equip shine, blurred shadows) are not drawn. Measurement (`UiFidelityGear.cs`: portrait and landscape detail, compare, settings and the battle-card `Strip`): detail and compare match in title, tab and button text size with panel offsets of 0, settings match in text size within 0-1 px of position (landscape at the same 26 px scroll) and the card interior is within 0-1 px. Remaining differences: `+ Add condition option` is drawn in Noto because of its full-width plus, and the inset shadow bands and the button shine are missing. Nine new strings were added to `en.txt` in the mockup's English. The mockup's `Run` and `Closed` boards (the sanctuary hub during a run, a closed window) have no matching window and were not built.\n\n## Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)\n\nThe training boards (`training` canvas, 1600 px: lobby, add-enemy dialog, result faster/slower/first clear/failed) differ in structure from the old window (two columns, a hero picture and drop-down tier/count), so the lobby, the add-enemy dialog and the result were redrawn in the mockup pixels. `TrainingGroundWindow` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`TrainingGroundWindow.Look.cs` holds the text, spacing and big-number shrinking, `.Lobby.cs` the lobby body, `.Picker.cs` the add-enemy dialog) and draws the skill rows (checkbox, picture, edict-edit button), the boss/normal enemy rows (four tier buttons `training-tier-<key>-<tier>`, count minus/value/plus `training-count-<key>-minus|plus|value`), the rift tier slider and the start button as laid out in the mockup. `TrainingGroundResultWindow` is three columns (PC), two (landscape phone) or one scrolling column over a pinned footer (portrait phone), and draws the clear time, the comparison box against the previous run (faster/slower/same/first record/failed copy), the DPS chart (this run and the previous one, cast marks in `DPS chart`), the damage-by-skill rows (normal/boss bars, uses, edict edit), the changed Hunt Edict list and the change-setup/restart footer. Game rules and records (`TrainingGround.*`, `RecordTrainingGround`) and most control names are unchanged; the drop-downs `training-tier-<key>`/`training-count-<key>` are now group objects with the buttons named above (`RuntimeTrainingGroundSmoke` updated). On the result, `victory-skill-N`, `Skill uses` and `Result summary` are gone and `training-back`, `training-result-skill-<id>` and `training-result-basic` are new. The phone boards carry a sample notch and home bar, so the driver (`UiFidelityTraining.cs`) simulates the same insets through `UiSafeArea.Simulate`. Measured: lobby and dialog texts are within 0 to 0.5 px of font size and 0 to 3 px of position (apart from rows that differ between the mockup sample data and real data); the three result states match in title, time, box, chart and row font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px, with vertical positions following the row count. The 20 new strings are filled in `en.txt` with the mockup English (10K/100M/T are only used by a Korean-only branch) and 8 unused old strings were removed. Remaining difference: the big time digits and the logo letter-spacing are slightly narrower in the Jua subset font.\n\n## Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)\n\nThe codex boards (`codex` canvas: 956 px landscape and 440 px portrait, list, normal detail, boss detail) are also drawn at 956 px wide on PC, so `MonsterCodexWindow` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`UiMock.WindowScale(safe,956)`). `MonsterCodexWindow.Look.cs` holds the text line boxes, the sky, the masthead (back button `codex-back`, logo, gilt title, `codex-crumb` in the detail), the progress bar `codex-progress`, the list (8 landscape / 4 portrait columns of `codex-card-<id>` cards with portrait frame, ID chip, boss ribbon and kill count) and the horizontally scrolling field tabs `codex-tab-<f>`; `MonsterCodexWindow.Detail.cs` holds the detail (identity box, life/attack/speed `codex-life|attack|speed` with notes, damage types, defence cells `codex-defense-<i>`, the skills panel `codex-skills` with rows, tag chips and boss skill icons, the rift tier bar `codex-tier-*` with slider, best-clear marker and the Normal/Elite `codex-grade` control or the boss tier chip). The portrait detail scrolls as one page. Game rules (`MonsterCodex.*`, tier and elite preview) and most control names are unchanged and the old `MonsterCodexWindow.Skin.cs` is gone. The chip and rule colours were added as `UiTheme.MockChipWash/MockChipInk/MockRowRule`. The driver `UiFidelityCodex.cs` captures the list, the 8th normal monster and the first boss at the same screen sizes with an account that has kill counts. Measured: font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px and positions within 0 to 3 px (apart from mockup sample versus real monster/skill text lengths and field tab widths). The 7 new strings are filled in `en.txt`. Remaining differences: logo letter-spacing width, tab height 36 px (below the 44 px touch rule, as in the mockup) and integer font-size rounding of 0.5 px. The native `RuntimeMonsterCodexSmoke` was adapted but not run.\n\n## Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)\n\nThe aspect stone boards (`aspect` canvas, 32 boards: imprint, growth, max level, uncollected, not eligible, empty search, already imprinted, replace/new confirmation, completion, guide, gear picker, the three portrait steps) differ in structure from the old window (one card list and an imprint button below), so the whole window was redrawn in the mockup pixels. `AspectStoneSession` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`UiMock.WindowScale(safe,1440)`); the board sizes live in `AspectLook` (PC, landscape and portrait columns), the masthead, service bar, phone step strip and docked action bar in `AspectStoneWindow.Chrome.cs`, the three-panel workbench (worn gear, aspect codex, imprint preview) in `.Panels.cs` and `.Detail.cs`, the popups in `.Dialogs.cs`. Worn gear is ten slots (`aspect-slot-<slot>-<index>`) around the character figure; the codex has the search box, the class drop-down (`aspect-class`), four state tabs (`aspect-filter-0..3`: all, collected, uncollected, upgrade) and 5/4/3 columns of cards (`aspect-select-<id>`); the preview draws the five level boxes, the level-up button (`aspect-upgrade`), the source, the current/new aspect comparison (`aspect-compare`) and the notes. The imprint confirmation and its completion stay separate windows on the shared host (`aspect-confirm-commit/cancel`, `aspect-success-close`); the portrait phone uses the step strip (`aspect-step-1..3`) and the gear picker (`aspect-equipment-picker`, `aspect-equip-<id>`). Game rules (`AspectStone.*`, `UpgradeAspect`, `ImprintAspect`) are unchanged. The mockup's \"In the field\" tab, demo footer and salvage demo modal are features the game does not have and are not drawn; the mockup has no close control, so `content-close` was added at the end of the service bar. The driver `UiFidelityAspect.cs` captures imprint, growth, low, max, locked, ineligible, empty, already, replace, confirm, success, help, picker, gear and detail at the same screen sizes with a nine-piece gear set and codex data (landscape uses `Simulate(53,27,53,8)`, portrait `Simulate(0,26,0,52)`). Measured: PC and portrait font sizes match exactly and positions are within 0 to 3 px (apart from row counts and font weight); landscape masthead, service bar and detail panel are within 0 to 1 px. The 48 new strings are filled in `en.txt` with the mockup English (the guide and part of the confirmation have no English board, so they were translated by hand). Remaining differences: the mockup's portrait step labels collapse into two squeezed lines that overlap the number disc (a CSS bug), so they are drawn as one centred line; the mockup's narrow landscape English folds the \"Equipped gear\" header onto two lines and pushes everything below by 17 px, which is not reproduced; the runestone SVG is replaced by the game's existing rune art. The native `RuntimeAspectStoneSmoke` was adapted but not run.\n\n## Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)\n\nCharacter creation and selection (`Docs/Design/CharacterPlaza`, 28 boards) is a full-screen scene with painted heroes, not a content window. `CharacterSelectionView` is already built from the mockup's `layout()` numbers (design unit = mockup px through `PixelScale`), so the back button, heading, play button, crests, name tag and badge match. Reading the code against `layout()` line by line, only the differing values were changed: the hint pill (landscape width 320 and height 22, PC height 22, portrait 26, landscape type 12.5), the landscape badge right limit (W−112), the play type size (landscape 22), and the gear popup (panel top 150/90/58, grid side 18/21/22, gap 12/14/12, top 96/100/84, cell height 118/150/112, icon 52/60/48, name and slot type sizes, title and hero line positions, close position, a vertically centred stack inside each cell). The hero placement and animation on the scene were not touched. The screen only exists inside a signed-in `GameUI` session, so no editor capture driver was written, and `RuntimeCharacterSelectionSmoke` was not run; the visual check of this screen is still open.\n\n## Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)\n\nThe rune board boards (`runeboard` canvas, 32 boards) are a new visual system: a light hex board with coloured blocks, six weapon cards, a mastery strip, storage and selection panels, and the practice, guide, preset and region dialogs, while the game's `RuneBoardWindow` uses the dark stone board art and `U`-unit layout. `UiFidelityRuneBoard.cs` captured the PC, landscape and portrait baselines: its type is 1.3 to 1.4 times the mockup's and the board art itself differs, so this is not a handful of numbers but a separate job that rebuilds the board drawing (`RuneBoardGraphic`) and every dialog. It risks the rune editing, tutorial and practice flows, so it was not redrawn in this pass.\n\n## Not audited yet\n\nBattle HUD graphs and observation menu (part of the whole battle HUD), redrawing the rune board (see the baseline above), and a visual check of the character selection screen. Download each canvas from Claude Design (procedure in `tools/ui_fidelity/README.md`) and measure it the same way.\n\n## Verification\n\nShared HUD, plaza and battle HUD: focused EditMode 112 tests (GlobalHudTests, CasualHudSharedTests, SharedUiTests, TownPlazaLayoutTests, TownWalkTests, CasualBattleHudTests, GlobalHudResourceTests, HudInspectionCasualTests) passed, daily quests and attendance: focused EditMode (DailyQuestSkinTests, AttendanceArtTests, AttendanceTests, LocalizationTests: 66 tests) passed, shared notices: 16 tests (CasualNoticeLayoutTests), town plaza layout: 19 tests (TownPlazaLayoutTests), rune master: focused EditMode 8 tests (RuneMasterCasualSkinTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed. Warehouse: focused EditMode 89 tests (StorageCasualSkinTests, StorageTests, UiButtonTests, EquipmentStorageTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed. Rift entry: focused EditMode 66 tests (RiftEntryCasualWindowTests, RiftEntryTests, LocalizationTests, CasualImportedAssetTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed, the native smokes were adapted but not run. Full EditMode, macOS player, devices and performance were not run.\n\n## Next\n\nAudit the windows under \"Not audited yet\" the same way (each needs an Editor driver `UiFidelity*.cs`; windows tied to GameUI are driven like `UiFidelityPlaza`, filling the fields without a `GameController` run). The skill tree already has a golden check with zero position and size difference.\n", "html": "<p><a href=\"#/page/casual-ui-fidelity-audit-20261020\">한국어<\/a><\/p><h2 id=\"method\">Method<\/h2><p>Every board of a mockup canvas (Claude Design) is rendered on a local server and every text, box and image rect is extracted in board pixels (<code>tools/ui_fidelity/measure.html<\/code>). The same values are extracted from the real window at the same size (a floating Unity Game View resized to 440×956 / 956×440 / 1600×900, <code>UiFidelityDump<\/code>) and compared with <code>compare.py<\/code> (text matched by string, boxes by IoU). Boards: portrait 440×956, phone landscape 956×440, PC 1600×900.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-cause-and-shared-fix\">Shared cause and shared fix<\/h2><ul><li>Earlier windows used game units (<code>UiTheme.Scale<\/code>) with ad-hoc constants that followed the mockup &quot;in proportion&quot; only: on the same board text was about 2 px small and cells, tabs and gaps differed.<\/li><li><code>UiMock<\/code> (board class + <code>UiTheme.Scale / reference scale<\/code>) makes one canvas unit equal one mockup pixel on the reference board and scales the board proportionally elsewhere. A window uses the mockup CSS tokens (<code>--cell<\/code>, <code>--head<\/code>, <code>--fs<\/code> …) directly.<\/li><li><code>StorageSurface.shadowOutside<\/code> and an extended button chrome hang the box-shadow outside the box, so the layout rect equals the mockup border-box.<\/li><li><code>EquipmentSlotView.Paint(…, CellRecipe)<\/code> draws the inner slot layout (art 86% at 7%/5%, level .17, chips .14/.2/.22) proportionally; other windows keep their look without a recipe.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"warehouse-applied\">Warehouse (applied)<\/h2><p>Measured against the old window (portrait): title 22.8→25, tab numerals 8.7→11, category 10.9→13, footer currency 11.9→16 px, cell 56.5→52, vault tab 38→46, plus category row, well, rail and footer positions now follow the mockup. Final measurement: normal and bulk states on all three boards are within 0.5 px in font size (integer font rounding) and mostly within 2 px in box position and size; only the PC tool chips and the usage pill are off by up to about 5–9 px. Dialogs match in width and font size; the expand dialog matches the 382 px frame height, help and open-tab are within ±5 px. The grade popover matches in position and was +14 px tall; that was corrected but not re-measured.<\/p><p>Known remaining gaps: drag ghost, pause pill and toast boards are not measured yet. Detail and preset dialog internals belong to shared parts (<code>ItemDetailView<\/code>, <code>CharacterEquipmentView<\/code>). The brand letter spacing is drawn by <code>UiTextSpacing<\/code> but the dump reads glyph width before the effect; the usage pill width differs from the mockup sample (&quot;12 / 60&quot;) with the count string.<\/p><h2 id=\"rune-master-applied\">Rune master (applied)<\/h2><p><code>RuneMasterWindow.Layout.cs<\/code> draws the reshape and ascend tabs in the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels (per-board head, footer, chips, slots, material cells and the fuse button). Final measurement: the three reshape boards and the PC idle/pair/error states differ in only 1–2 of 12–13 texts (the brand letter-spacing width and a 4% narrower long note on the landscape board), and portrait box deviation is zero. Real differences fixed in this pass: footer skip-button height (landscape +4, portrait +2), landscape ascend group row height 51→53, the ascend summary line height now uses the mockup&#x27;s fractional size instead of the rounded font, two-digit grade chip counts wrapping on portrait, and the initial scrollbar position. Remaining: the bottom empty bag cells on PC/landscape look shorter in the dump only because the viewport clips them (raw height 91.6 matches); the usage pill differs by about 3 px with digit width; the ascend board is driven with a different group state than each mockup board (each board registers a different cell count), so the registered-materials row is matched by formula (material and result cell heights) only.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-confirm-and-toast-applied\">Shared confirm and toast (applied)<\/h2><p><code>CasualNoticeLayout<\/code> now computes the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels times <code>UiMock.CanvasScale<\/code> in physical pixels (<code>For(safe, scale)<\/code>, the device value is <code>Current()<\/code>); before, it used raw <code>Screen<\/code> pixels and drew at about a third of the mockup on a DPR 3 phone. The card is border-box like the mockup (the border sits outside the padding); the toast groups portrait with landscape for border, shadow, padding and the 9 px gap (<code>.o-land .tst,.o-port .tst<\/code>), the info badge and text are one centred group, and the badge is drawn as the mockup&#x27;s white disc, navy ring and blue &quot;i&quot; (<code>CasualNoticeInfo<\/code>) instead of the old sprite. Measured on three boards: font size difference 0, text position within 3 px, toast box size equal. The toast&#x27;s vertical place follows the live HUD top and was not measured. At the 1:1 editor board text is about 4% narrower because legacy <code>Text<\/code> rounds glyph advances to whole pixels; at device scale that falls below 1%.<\/p><h2 id=\"town-plaza-partly-applied\">Town plaza (partly applied)<\/h2><p>The existing build already matched font sizes and most box positions. Fixed in this pass: the interaction card detail now starts on its first line (+10 px), the talk button carries the mockup&#x27;s chat glyph as a centred row, the title plate&#x27;s 3 px shadow is part of its rect, and the PC guide panel uses the 3 px border inset and 556 px height. Kept on purpose: the top-right cog, content dock and event button follow PR #144 (2026-10-18, &quot;shortcuts beneath a painted cog&quot;) and the <code>menu-*<\/code> medallion art, so they differ from the mockup&#x27;s flat cog and dock place. The shared HUD text sizes and the portrait slot row were matched under &quot;Shared HUD&quot; below. The place name contains a glyph outside the Jua subset (&quot;잿&quot;), so it falls back to Noto and is about 9 px wider. The card and name tags are enlarged by a transform scale (cardScale/scale), so on small canvases (0.54 on portrait) their text is rasterised at canvas resolution and magnified (may look soft, follow-up).<\/p><h2 id=\"daily-quests-and-attendance-applied\">Daily quests and attendance (applied)<\/h2><p>The three pages are redrawn on <code>EventsPlate<\/code> (the mockup&#x27;s cream plate with masthead, three tabs and footer strip) and <code>ContentWindowView<\/code>&#x27;s fitted mode (<code>Open(..., fit)<\/code>, <code>RepaintFitted<\/code> draws only the scrim and one plate). Every size is the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels; <code>UiMock.WindowScale<\/code> (scale from the whole screen) and <code>UiMock.Plate<\/code> (safe area less 8 px, PC capped at 1500×810) place the plate. The old build stretched with the screen aspect. Final measurement: daily quests have 32/37 (portrait) and 37/43 (PC) texts at the mockup&#x27;s size and place (the rest are ≤4% width differences and glyphs outside the Jua subset), 7-day attendance 21/21 on PC and 28-day 17/17. The tab waiting dots, footer strips, remaining time (HH:MM:SS) and claim toast follow the mockup. Because of the mockup&#x27;s <code>.dq button{margin:0;font:inherit;color:inherit}<\/code>, the DOM draws tab and claim text as navy Noto 14 px and the title as gilt Jua, so the DOM dump was the reference. Remaining: the dump cannot read the shadow (UiWindowFrame +5 px) or clipped scroll content, and the daily claim/refresh toast and the closed states were not measured.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-hud-applied\">Shared HUD (applied)<\/h2><p><code>GlobalHudLayout.HudFont<\/code> sets the level, HP, resource, EXP, caption and cooldown text to the mockup&#x27;s per-board size (PC, landscape, portrait), and the portrait slot row baseline (147) and shortcut captions (below the slots) follow the mockup. Checked on plaza HUD captures: Lv 17/13/13, HP and resource 13/10/10, EXP and captions 15/11/11, cooldown numbers 26/15/17 (PC/portrait/landscape). The plaza and battle HUD mockups disagree by about 1 px on HUD text and on the portrait slot size (46 vs 42); the game follows the plaza mockup.<\/p><h2 id=\"hunt-edict-window-legacy-partly-applied\">Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)<\/h2><p>The legacy <code>HuntEdictWindow<\/code> is about to be removed, so work stopped there on the user&#x27;s instruction. Applied: the mockup&#x27;s border-box frame (padding inside the 3 px border, header, rule and tab spacing, a dashed footer rule with the buttons), drop shadows hanging outside the rect so control sizes equal the mockup boxes (<code>UiButtonFace.ShadowOutside<\/code>, <code>HuntEdictSurface.shadowOutside<\/code>), the header subtitle pill, tab icon and label placement, the overview switch row and style cards, the group editor (search row, group chips, white section card, quick-preset stack, option rows), the skill manage list, the equipped-skills dock, the policy page header, preset tabs and the rail dock. Measured: on PC the combat, loot, bag, repeat and explore (custom rows) tabs match almost every text in size and place, with 1-2 box deviations. What remains is the Noto fallback for strings with the middle dot (not in the Jua subset, 3 to 14 px wider) and <code>.5<\/code> font sizes rounding. Not done: presets, recommended equipment, dialogs and the survival popups (hazard canvas) - skipped because they are legacy. Found on the way: the window&#x27;s palette fields read <code>Resources<\/code> in a <code>MonoBehaviour<\/code> field initializer, which made the first text transparent; they are now read on use.<\/p><h2 id=\"settings-window-applied\">Settings window (applied)<\/h2><p>The settings canvas used <code>ApplyScaler(..., *.5)<\/code>, which drew it about 20% smaller than the mockup on phones; it now uses <code>UiMock.CanvasScale<\/code> (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel) and a per-board table <code>SettingsLook<\/code> (padding, gap, font, tab height, chip, button, bar, seal) for the frame (title, close, rule, tab row, scrolling body), card padding, section headers, body and muted copy (with line height, <code>SettingsParagraph<\/code>), notices, sliders (bar <code>barh<\/code>, 17 px marker, ticks), choice cards (map, language, hero), buttons and the UID strip. The 12 px body scrollbar takes width only when content overflows; the audio tab uses volume rows (no gap) and a mute card (checkbox and two lines); the character tab holds the hero cards in one card. Measured: PC screen tab 17 of 19 texts and language 11 of 12 match; audio, character and account have the structure and 7-9 texts at the same size and place. Remaining: the value <code>%<\/code> drawn apart from the number, the audio bar markers, account UID and pending counts (demo data), notice line counts (text width); the guide and combat-state tabs use the same table but were not measured separately. <code>CasualSettingsTests<\/code> now measure in mockup units (tab width and height, UID strip, slider touch area).<\/p><h2 id=\"equipment-shop-applied-pc-board-1440x810\">Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)<\/h2><p>The mockup (<code>Equipment shop - casual<\/code>) differs from the old screen in structure, so the window was redrawn in the mockup&#x27;s pixels instead of retuning values (<code>EquipmentShopWindow.Mock*.cs<\/code>; the gamble merchant and the auto-selection settings shared with salvage keep their old layout). One canvas unit is one mockup pixel and the PC board is 1440 wide, hence the new <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code>. Built new: the header (brand, place, title, close), the tab row with the restock countdown, rarity and order selects (small list popups) and the category strip, 56 px offer rows with the &quot;Small upgrade&quot; tag, the inventory grid (8 columns, 50 slots, categories, the multi-select bar, the auto-select notice band), two comparison popups for buy, sell and buyback (deltas, lost properties, upgrade record, fact table) and the dock under them, the footer (gold, gems, status, History, Help), thin scrollbars that take width only when content overflows, the dialog frame (sale confirmation, item details, presets, history, help) and the auto-selection settings (rarity chips, equipment kinds, details, exclusion rules). History is backed by <code>EquipmentShopState.ledger<\/code> (the last 30 purchases, sales and buybacks, newest first; an additive save field, old saves load an empty list). At the user&#x27;s request the item picture in the comparison popups is larger than the mockup&#x27;s 40 px (72 px on PC, 52 px on phones). Measured: on the PC buy, sell, multi-sale, auto-select settings and details boards texts and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px and control sizes match (grid cell 63 px, offer row 56 px, popup 425.5 px). Remaining: the middle dot missing from the Jua subset makes the title and place strings fall back to Noto (3 to 14 px wider) and Jua digits are about 10% wider, no &quot;Unique&quot; rarity chip (a reserved legacy bit that cannot be selected), the mockup&#x27;s EN/KO toggle is left to the language setting, and the enlarged item picture (requested). 60 new strings carry English in <code>en.txt<\/code>.<\/p><h2 id=\"blacksmith-applied-pc-board-1440x810\">Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)<\/h2><p>The blacksmith mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Blacksmith - casual<\/code>) differs from the old screen in structure (free-floating round header buttons, five pill tabs, rounded panel cards, a docked action bar), so it was redrawn in the mockup&#x27;s unit <code>U<\/code> (PC 14 px, phone landscape 12.32 px, phone portrait 13.86 px). Built new: the shared frame (header, tabs, wallets, lock tip, sealed screen, toast), option change (item list, option rows, selection dock), slot enhancement (slot rows, work status, workshop, growth table, unlock popup), gear enhancement (item list, growth steps, quote dock, auto-enhance settings and result, confirm popup), core crafting (core equipment panel, catalogue, forge, quality gauge, result and history popups), the item picker sheet and the help dialog. Files are split into <code>BlacksmithWindow.<em>Screen.cs<\/code>, <code><\/em>Dialogs.cs<\/code>, <code>Modal.cs<\/code>, <code>Overlays.cs<\/code> and <code>CoresEquip.cs<\/code>; the capture driver is <code>UiFidelityForge.cs<\/code> (specs enchant/slots/gear/cores with <code>+confirm<\/code>, <code>+hit<\/code>, <code>+complete<\/code>, <code>+workshop<\/code>, <code>+unlock<\/code>, <code>+plan<\/code>, <code>+core-*<\/code>, <code>sealed<\/code>, <code>locktip<\/code>). Measured: on the PC, phone landscape and phone portrait boards text sizes match and text and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px; only a few knife-edge line breaks differ because Jua is about 10% wider. The mockup&#x27;s demo-only history and reset popups were not built (the game has no reroll history). New strings carry English in <code>en.txt<\/code> and the old ones were pruned.<\/p><h2 id=\"jeweler-applied-pc-and-phone-boards\">Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)<\/h2><p>The jeweler mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Jeweler - casual<\/code>) differs from the old window (three tabs, gem list, potion rows), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel, <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code>). Its CSS answers the board size with container queries, so <code>JewelerLook<\/code> starts from the base rules and overrides them in the stylesheet&#x27;s own order (wide &gt;=1550, mid 681-1100, short &lt;=600, mobile &lt;=680). Built new: the masthead (gem emblem, gilt title, active elixir, tier values, sockets, close), the service bar, the 6x6 owned-gem matrix with the inspected gem and tier conversion (1, 5, all), six gem tabs and six elixir cards (three effects, owned, state), the docked brew bar (quantity minus/plus/max input and the brew button), the footer note, the toast and the tier values dialog. Game-side changes: <code>Jeweler.Craft(count)<\/code> and <code>CraftLimit<\/code> let the player brew any quantity (the <code>GemBatch<\/code> versions are unchanged); the elixir pictures use the game&#x27;s approved elixir art in the same box instead of the mockup&#x27;s SVG vials. The mockup&#x27;s demo-only parts (the &quot;try elixir&quot; button, the reset dialog, the draft note) were not built; the close button, the sockets button and the broken-gem purchase row were added because the game needs them. <code>MockKit<\/code> is the new shared pixel helper (text boxes, cards, press, icons, thin scrolling) the next windows reuse. Measured (<code>UiFidelityJeweler.cs<\/code>: brew, convert, empty, crafted, balance, with <code>@inv|@pot|@body|@potions<\/code> scroll variants): on PC 1440x810 and 1440x900, phone landscape and phone portrait every text size matches and positions sit within 0-2 px (brew dock, matrix, cards and dialog included). Remaining: the description paragraph sits 1-2 px low, dashed outlines of empty cells, the game&#x27;s elixir art instead of the SVG vials, wrap widths of the shield and long sentences (Unity breaks Hangul lines differently); the wide (&gt;=1550) and short (601-760 tall) rules were transcribed from the CSS but could not be measured. New strings carry the mockup&#x27;s English in <code>en.txt<\/code> (&quot;Jeweler (casual)&quot;) and 21 old entries were pruned.<\/p><h2 id=\"rift-entry-applied-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards-plus-eight-modals\">Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)<\/h2><p>The rift entry mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Rift Entry - casual<\/code>) differs from the old window (title row, scrolling body, bottom buttons), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels. <code>RiftEntryWindow.Fit<\/code> uses <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code> to make one canvas unit one mockup pixel (the boards are full-bleed, no safe area) and takes the mockup&#x27;s <code>--b<\/code> (PC/landscape <code>clamp(9, min(1.4583%W, 2.9167%H), 24)<\/code>, portrait <code>min(3.5%W, 1.842%H)<\/code>) as the one unit every size is a multiple of. Wide boards use three columns (art .95/3 · fatigue, potions, skills · entry), portrait stacks them and shares the spare height as <code>(H-content)/4<\/code> gaps (at least .3B). New: the masthead (back, logo, divider, title, abyssal coins, close), the portal art panel with tier picker, record and first-clear chest, the fatigue gauge (daily and restored spans, reset time, carry-over), three equipped-potion rows, five skill tiles (four actives and the ultimate), the services row, three entry buttons (1x, 1.5x, resume) and the help button. Modals follow the mockup&#x27;s <code>.modal<\/code> rules (wide, narrow &lt;=650, short &lt;=570) through <code>ModalLook<\/code> and are drawn into their own fixed views: 1.5x confirm, fatigue recovery confirm, fatigue help, potion picker (six elixir tabs plus an ordinary-potion tab, six tier cards), skill/ultimate picker (three-column option cards, details box, Close/Replace) and first-clear rewards (virtualized 1,000-tier list). Game rules and saves are unchanged (<code>Store.ReplaceRiftSkill<\/code>, <code>game.SelectRiftPotion<\/code> and the fatigue paths are the same) and the window keeps its old control names (<code>rift-enter-fast<\/code>, <code>rift-skill-N<\/code>, <code>rift-potion-N<\/code>, ...). The two portal paintings (tall and wide) were generated with Codex and installed under <code>Resources/Art/RiftEntry/Casual/<\/code> (record: <code>Docs/Art/RiftEntryPortal20261020/<\/code>, model not reported, the generator&#x27;s own check flagged the composition, so not production-approved). Measurement (<code>UiFidelityRift.cs<\/code>: lobby, newtier, claimed, empty, mixed, limit, poor and the modals fast, recover, help, potion, potiondiamond, skill, ultimate, rewards): the three lobby boards and the confirm, recover and help modals match in text size with positions inside 0-1 px; the potion, skill, ultimate and rewards modals are within +-0.5 px in text size (legacy Text uses whole sizes) and 0-2 px in position. Deliberate differences from the mockup: the potion tabs gain an &quot;Ordinary potion&quot; tab the mockup lacks (ordinary potions stay equippable), the reward rows show the real reward chest art with claim state and unlock lines instead of the empty placeholder and the &quot;next content&quot; hint moved from the lobby to the top of that modal, and the skill details append rank, damage, cost and activation order (real data) under the mockup&#x27;s one-line description, so the body scrolls on short boards. Titles containing a middle dot (.) are drawn in Noto because of the Jua glyph limit. 38 new strings were added to <code>en.txt<\/code> in the mockup&#x27;s English and four changed ones (Replace skill, Enter Rift, Change potion, the tagline) were aligned to it. The native smokes (<code>RuntimeRiftEntrySmoke*<\/code> and friends) were adapted to the new structure (fixed view, modal names, virtual list) but not run this time.<\/p><h2 id=\"rift-gear-offer-applied-portrait-440x956-and-landscape-956x440-boards\">Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)<\/h2><p>The gear offer mockup (<code>Rift gear offer UI - casual<\/code>: battle cards, details, compare and settings) differs from the old windows (a header/tabs/scroll shell hosting the shared <code>ItemDetailView<\/code> and <code>EquipmentComparisonView<\/code>), so the detail/compare and settings windows were redrawn in mockup pixels. <code>RiftRecommendationWindow<\/code> uses <code>RiftEntryWindow.Fit<\/code> (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel; cards, rows and cells live in <code>RiftRecommendationWindow.Look.cs<\/code>) and draws the compare tab, the ring target choice (left/right), the worn and recommended cards, the score line (old -&gt; new), the real stat rows (base, fixed resistance, affixes, special effects, set bonuses) and the pinned Equip/Close row. <code>RiftGearSettingsWindow<\/code> draws the numbered sections (I-IV), pill choices, per-position detail panels (threshold fields, add-option list), the time slider and the pinned Save/Close row as in the mockup; the landscape board is a 560x424 window whose body alone scrolls (the mockup shows it scrolled by 26 px, so it matches at the same offset). The battle cards (<code>GameUI.GearRecommendations<\/code>) follow the mockup card rules (inner 1 px border, radius 12, shadow outside): the card is 92x150, the score pill 80x17, the Equip button 58x20 and the gear/close buttons 24 px, all at the mockup positions. Game rules and transactions (<code>EquipInventoryItem<\/code>, <code>SaveRiftGearOfferSettings<\/code>) and the old control names are unchanged, and the real item and setting values replace the mockup&#x27;s samples in the same row look (a longer real stat list makes the body scroll). Decorations only the mockup has (the Equip shine, blurred shadows) are not drawn. Measurement (<code>UiFidelityGear.cs<\/code>: portrait and landscape detail, compare, settings and the battle-card <code>Strip<\/code>): detail and compare match in title, tab and button text size with panel offsets of 0, settings match in text size within 0-1 px of position (landscape at the same 26 px scroll) and the card interior is within 0-1 px. Remaining differences: <code>+ Add condition option<\/code> is drawn in Noto because of its full-width plus, and the inset shadow bands and the button shine are missing. Nine new strings were added to <code>en.txt<\/code> in the mockup&#x27;s English. The mockup&#x27;s <code>Run<\/code> and <code>Closed<\/code> boards (the sanctuary hub during a run, a closed window) have no matching window and were not built.<\/p><h2 id=\"training-ground-applied-lobby-add-enemy-dialog-and-three-result-states-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards\">Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)<\/h2><p>The training boards (<code>training<\/code> canvas, 1600 px: lobby, add-enemy dialog, result faster/slower/first clear/failed) differ in structure from the old window (two columns, a hero picture and drop-down tier/count), so the lobby, the add-enemy dialog and the result were redrawn in the mockup pixels. <code>TrainingGroundWindow<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>TrainingGroundWindow.Look.cs<\/code> holds the text, spacing and big-number shrinking, <code>.Lobby.cs<\/code> the lobby body, <code>.Picker.cs<\/code> the add-enemy dialog) and draws the skill rows (checkbox, picture, edict-edit button), the boss/normal enemy rows (four tier buttons <code>training-tier-&lt;key&gt;-&lt;tier&gt;<\/code>, count minus/value/plus <code>training-count-&lt;key&gt;-minus|plus|value<\/code>), the rift tier slider and the start button as laid out in the mockup. <code>TrainingGroundResultWindow<\/code> is three columns (PC), two (landscape phone) or one scrolling column over a pinned footer (portrait phone), and draws the clear time, the comparison box against the previous run (faster/slower/same/first record/failed copy), the DPS chart (this run and the previous one, cast marks in <code>DPS chart<\/code>), the damage-by-skill rows (normal/boss bars, uses, edict edit), the changed Hunt Edict list and the change-setup/restart footer. Game rules and records (<code>TrainingGround.*<\/code>, <code>RecordTrainingGround<\/code>) and most control names are unchanged; the drop-downs <code>training-tier-&lt;key&gt;<\/code>/<code>training-count-&lt;key&gt;<\/code> are now group objects with the buttons named above (<code>RuntimeTrainingGroundSmoke<\/code> updated). On the result, <code>victory-skill-N<\/code>, <code>Skill uses<\/code> and <code>Result summary<\/code> are gone and <code>training-back<\/code>, <code>training-result-skill-&lt;id&gt;<\/code> and <code>training-result-basic<\/code> are new. The phone boards carry a sample notch and home bar, so the driver (<code>UiFidelityTraining.cs<\/code>) simulates the same insets through <code>UiSafeArea.Simulate<\/code>. Measured: lobby and dialog texts are within 0 to 0.5 px of font size and 0 to 3 px of position (apart from rows that differ between the mockup sample data and real data); the three result states match in title, time, box, chart and row font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px, with vertical positions following the row count. The 20 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code> with the mockup English (10K/100M/T are only used by a Korean-only branch) and 8 unused old strings were removed. Remaining difference: the big time digits and the logo letter-spacing are slightly narrower in the Jua subset font.<\/p><h2 id=\"monster-codex-applied-list-normal-detail-and-boss-detail-landscape-and-portrait-boards\">Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)<\/h2><p>The codex boards (<code>codex<\/code> canvas: 956 px landscape and 440 px portrait, list, normal detail, boss detail) are also drawn at 956 px wide on PC, so <code>MonsterCodexWindow<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe,956)<\/code>). <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Look.cs<\/code> holds the text line boxes, the sky, the masthead (back button <code>codex-back<\/code>, logo, gilt title, <code>codex-crumb<\/code> in the detail), the progress bar <code>codex-progress<\/code>, the list (8 landscape / 4 portrait columns of <code>codex-card-&lt;id&gt;<\/code> cards with portrait frame, ID chip, boss ribbon and kill count) and the horizontally scrolling field tabs <code>codex-tab-&lt;f&gt;<\/code>; <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Detail.cs<\/code> holds the detail (identity box, life/attack/speed <code>codex-life|attack|speed<\/code> with notes, damage types, defence cells <code>codex-defense-&lt;i&gt;<\/code>, the skills panel <code>codex-skills<\/code> with rows, tag chips and boss skill icons, the rift tier bar <code>codex-tier-<em><\/code> with slider, best-clear marker and the Normal/Elite <code>codex-grade<\/code> control or the boss tier chip). The portrait detail scrolls as one page. Game rules (<code>MonsterCodex.<\/em><\/code>, tier and elite preview) and most control names are unchanged and the old <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Skin.cs<\/code> is gone. The chip and rule colours were added as <code>UiTheme.MockChipWash/MockChipInk/MockRowRule<\/code>. The driver <code>UiFidelityCodex.cs<\/code> captures the list, the 8th normal monster and the first boss at the same screen sizes with an account that has kill counts. Measured: font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px and positions within 0 to 3 px (apart from mockup sample versus real monster/skill text lengths and field tab widths). The 7 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code>. Remaining differences: logo letter-spacing width, tab height 36 px (below the 44 px touch rule, as in the mockup) and integer font-size rounding of 0.5 px. The native <code>RuntimeMonsterCodexSmoke<\/code> was adapted but not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"aspect-stone-applied-pc-1440-phone-landscape-956-phone-portrait-440-boards-and-popups\">Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)<\/h2><p>The aspect stone boards (<code>aspect<\/code> canvas, 32 boards: imprint, growth, max level, uncollected, not eligible, empty search, already imprinted, replace/new confirmation, completion, guide, gear picker, the three portrait steps) differ in structure from the old window (one card list and an imprint button below), so the whole window was redrawn in the mockup pixels. <code>AspectStoneSession<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe,1440)<\/code>); the board sizes live in <code>AspectLook<\/code> (PC, landscape and portrait columns), the masthead, service bar, phone step strip and docked action bar in <code>AspectStoneWindow.Chrome.cs<\/code>, the three-panel workbench (worn gear, aspect codex, imprint preview) in <code>.Panels.cs<\/code> and <code>.Detail.cs<\/code>, the popups in <code>.Dialogs.cs<\/code>. Worn gear is ten slots (<code>aspect-slot-&lt;slot&gt;-&lt;index&gt;<\/code>) around the character figure; the codex has the search box, the class drop-down (<code>aspect-class<\/code>), four state tabs (<code>aspect-filter-0..3<\/code>: all, collected, uncollected, upgrade) and 5/4/3 columns of cards (<code>aspect-select-&lt;id&gt;<\/code>); the preview draws the five level boxes, the level-up button (<code>aspect-upgrade<\/code>), the source, the current/new aspect comparison (<code>aspect-compare<\/code>) and the notes. The imprint confirmation and its completion stay separate windows on the shared host (<code>aspect-confirm-commit/cancel<\/code>, <code>aspect-success-close<\/code>); the portrait phone uses the step strip (<code>aspect-step-1..3<\/code>) and the gear picker (<code>aspect-equipment-picker<\/code>, <code>aspect-equip-&lt;id&gt;<\/code>). Game rules (<code>AspectStone.*<\/code>, <code>UpgradeAspect<\/code>, <code>ImprintAspect<\/code>) are unchanged. The mockup&#x27;s &quot;In the field&quot; tab, demo footer and salvage demo modal are features the game does not have and are not drawn; the mockup has no close control, so <code>content-close<\/code> was added at the end of the service bar. The driver <code>UiFidelityAspect.cs<\/code> captures imprint, growth, low, max, locked, ineligible, empty, already, replace, confirm, success, help, picker, gear and detail at the same screen sizes with a nine-piece gear set and codex data (landscape uses <code>Simulate(53,27,53,8)<\/code>, portrait <code>Simulate(0,26,0,52)<\/code>). Measured: PC and portrait font sizes match exactly and positions are within 0 to 3 px (apart from row counts and font weight); landscape masthead, service bar and detail panel are within 0 to 1 px. The 48 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code> with the mockup English (the guide and part of the confirmation have no English board, so they were translated by hand). Remaining differences: the mockup&#x27;s portrait step labels collapse into two squeezed lines that overlap the number disc (a CSS bug), so they are drawn as one centred line; the mockup&#x27;s narrow landscape English folds the &quot;Equipped gear&quot; header onto two lines and pushes everything below by 17 px, which is not reproduced; the runestone SVG is replaced by the game&#x27;s existing rune art. The native <code>RuntimeAspectStoneSmoke<\/code> was adapted but not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"character-selection-screen-numeric-alignment-only-no-capture-measurement\">Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)<\/h2><p>Character creation and selection (<code>Docs/Design/CharacterPlaza<\/code>, 28 boards) is a full-screen scene with painted heroes, not a content window. <code>CharacterSelectionView<\/code> is already built from the mockup&#x27;s <code>layout()<\/code> numbers (design unit = mockup px through <code>PixelScale<\/code>), so the back button, heading, play button, crests, name tag and badge match. Reading the code against <code>layout()<\/code> line by line, only the differing values were changed: the hint pill (landscape width 320 and height 22, PC height 22, portrait 26, landscape type 12.5), the landscape badge right limit (W−112), the play type size (landscape 22), and the gear popup (panel top 150/90/58, grid side 18/21/22, gap 12/14/12, top 96/100/84, cell height 118/150/112, icon 52/60/48, name and slot type sizes, title and hero line positions, close position, a vertically centred stack inside each cell). The hero placement and animation on the scene were not touched. The screen only exists inside a signed-in <code>GameUI<\/code> session, so no editor capture driver was written, and <code>RuntimeCharacterSelectionSmoke<\/code> was not run; the visual check of this screen is still open.<\/p><h2 id=\"rune-board-measurement-baseline-only-not-redrawn\">Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)<\/h2><p>The rune board boards (<code>runeboard<\/code> canvas, 32 boards) are a new visual system: a light hex board with coloured blocks, six weapon cards, a mastery strip, storage and selection panels, and the practice, guide, preset and region dialogs, while the game&#x27;s <code>RuneBoardWindow<\/code> uses the dark stone board art and <code>U<\/code>-unit layout. <code>UiFidelityRuneBoard.cs<\/code> captured the PC, landscape and portrait baselines: its type is 1.3 to 1.4 times the mockup&#x27;s and the board art itself differs, so this is not a handful of numbers but a separate job that rebuilds the board drawing (<code>RuneBoardGraphic<\/code>) and every dialog. It risks the rune editing, tutorial and practice flows, so it was not redrawn in this pass.<\/p><h2 id=\"not-audited-yet\">Not audited yet<\/h2><p>Battle HUD graphs and observation menu (part of the whole battle HUD), redrawing the rune board (see the baseline above), and a visual check of the character selection screen. Download each canvas from Claude Design (procedure in <code>tools/ui_fidelity/README.md<\/code>) and measure it the same way.<\/p><h2 id=\"verification\">Verification<\/h2><p>Shared HUD, plaza and battle HUD: focused EditMode 112 tests (GlobalHudTests, CasualHudSharedTests, SharedUiTests, TownPlazaLayoutTests, TownWalkTests, CasualBattleHudTests, GlobalHudResourceTests, HudInspectionCasualTests) passed, daily quests and attendance: focused EditMode (DailyQuestSkinTests, AttendanceArtTests, AttendanceTests, LocalizationTests: 66 tests) passed, shared notices: 16 tests (CasualNoticeLayoutTests), town plaza layout: 19 tests (TownPlazaLayoutTests), rune master: focused EditMode 8 tests (RuneMasterCasualSkinTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed. Warehouse: focused EditMode 89 tests (StorageCasualSkinTests, StorageTests, UiButtonTests, EquipmentStorageTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed. Rift entry: focused EditMode 66 tests (RiftEntryCasualWindowTests, RiftEntryTests, LocalizationTests, CasualImportedAssetTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed, the native smokes were adapted but not run. Full EditMode, macOS player, devices and performance were not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"next\">Next<\/h2><p>Audit the windows under &quot;Not audited yet&quot; the same way (each needs an Editor driver <code>UiFidelity*.cs<\/code>; windows tied to GameUI are driven like <code>UiFidelityPlaza<\/code>, filling the fields without a <code>GameController<\/code> run). The skill tree already has a golden check with zero position and size difference.<\/p>", "headings": [{"id": "method", "title": "Method"}, {"id": "shared-cause-and-shared-fix", "title": "Shared cause and shared fix"}, {"id": "warehouse-applied", "title": "Warehouse (applied)"}, {"id": "rune-master-applied", "title": "Rune master (applied)"}, {"id": "shared-confirm-and-toast-applied", "title": "Shared confirm and toast (applied)"}, {"id": "town-plaza-partly-applied", "title": "Town plaza (partly applied)"}, {"id": "daily-quests-and-attendance-applied", "title": "Daily quests and attendance (applied)"}, {"id": "shared-hud-applied", "title": "Shared HUD (applied)"}, {"id": "hunt-edict-window-legacy-partly-applied", "title": "Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)"}, {"id": "settings-window-applied", "title": "Settings window (applied)"}, {"id": "equipment-shop-applied-pc-board-1440x810", "title": "Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)"}, {"id": "blacksmith-applied-pc-board-1440x810", "title": "Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)"}, {"id": "jeweler-applied-pc-and-phone-boards", "title": "Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)"}, {"id": "rift-entry-applied-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards-plus-eight-modals", "title": "Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)"}, {"id": "rift-gear-offer-applied-portrait-440x956-and-landscape-956x440-boards", "title": "Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)"}, {"id": "training-ground-applied-lobby-add-enemy-dialog-and-three-result-states-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards", "title": "Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)"}, {"id": "monster-codex-applied-list-normal-detail-and-boss-detail-landscape-and-portrait-boards", "title": "Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)"}, {"id": "aspect-stone-applied-pc-1440-phone-landscape-956-phone-portrait-440-boards-and-popups", "title": "Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)"}, {"id": "character-selection-screen-numeric-alignment-only-no-capture-measurement", "title": "Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)"}, {"id": "rune-board-measurement-baseline-only-not-redrawn", "title": "Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)"}, {"id": "not-audited-yet", "title": "Not audited yet"}, {"id": "verification", "title": "Verification"}, {"id": "next", "title": "Next"}]}, {"body": "# Casual UI mockup fidelity audit — 2026-10-20\n\n[한국어](Casual_UI_Fidelity_Audit_20261020.md)\n\n## Method\n\nEvery board of a mockup canvas (Claude Design) is rendered on a local server and every text, box and image rect is extracted in board pixels (`tools/ui_fidelity/measure.html`). The same values are extracted from the real window at the same size (a floating Unity Game View resized to 440×956 / 956×440 / 1600×900, `UiFidelityDump`) and compared with `compare.py` (text matched by string, boxes by IoU). Boards: portrait 440×956, phone landscape 956×440, PC 1600×900.\n\n## Shared cause and shared fix\n\n- Earlier windows used game units (`UiTheme.Scale`) with ad-hoc constants that followed the mockup \"in proportion\" only: on the same board text was about 2 px small and cells, tabs and gaps differed.\n- `UiMock` (board class + `UiTheme.Scale / reference scale`) makes one canvas unit equal one mockup pixel on the reference board and scales the board proportionally elsewhere. A window uses the mockup CSS tokens (`--cell`, `--head`, `--fs` …) directly.\n- `StorageSurface.shadowOutside` and an extended button chrome hang the box-shadow outside the box, so the layout rect equals the mockup border-box.\n- `EquipmentSlotView.Paint(…, CellRecipe)` draws the inner slot layout (art 86% at 7%/5%, level .17, chips .14/.2/.22) proportionally; other windows keep their look without a recipe.\n\n## Warehouse (applied)\n\nMeasured against the old window (portrait): title 22.8→25, tab numerals 8.7→11, category 10.9→13, footer currency 11.9→16 px, cell 56.5→52, vault tab 38→46, plus category row, well, rail and footer positions now follow the mockup. Final measurement: normal and bulk states on all three boards are within 0.5 px in font size (integer font rounding) and mostly within 2 px in box position and size; only the PC tool chips and the usage pill are off by up to about 5–9 px. Dialogs match in width and font size; the expand dialog matches the 382 px frame height, help and open-tab are within ±5 px. The grade popover matches in position and was +14 px tall; that was corrected but not re-measured.\n\nKnown remaining gaps: drag ghost, pause pill and toast boards are not measured yet. Detail and preset dialog internals belong to shared parts (`ItemDetailView`, `CharacterEquipmentView`). The brand letter spacing is drawn by `UiTextSpacing` but the dump reads glyph width before the effect; the usage pill width differs from the mockup sample (\"12 / 60\") with the count string.\n\n## Rune master (applied)\n\n`RuneMasterWindow.Layout.cs` draws the reshape and ascend tabs in the mockup's CSS pixels (per-board head, footer, chips, slots, material cells and the fuse button). Final measurement: the three reshape boards and the PC idle/pair/error states differ in only 1–2 of 12–13 texts (the brand letter-spacing width and a 4% narrower long note on the landscape board), and portrait box deviation is zero. Real differences fixed in this pass: footer skip-button height (landscape +4, portrait +2), landscape ascend group row height 51→53, the ascend summary line height now uses the mockup's fractional size instead of the rounded font, two-digit grade chip counts wrapping on portrait, and the initial scrollbar position. Remaining: the bottom empty bag cells on PC/landscape look shorter in the dump only because the viewport clips them (raw height 91.6 matches); the usage pill differs by about 3 px with digit width; the ascend board is driven with a different group state than each mockup board (each board registers a different cell count), so the registered-materials row is matched by formula (material and result cell heights) only.\n\n## Shared confirm and toast (applied)\n\n`CasualNoticeLayout` now computes the mockup's CSS pixels times `UiMock.CanvasScale` in physical pixels (`For(safe, scale)`, the device value is `Current()`); before, it used raw `Screen` pixels and drew at about a third of the mockup on a DPR 3 phone. The card is border-box like the mockup (the border sits outside the padding); the toast groups portrait with landscape for border, shadow, padding and the 9 px gap (`.o-land .tst,.o-port .tst`), the info badge and text are one centred group, and the badge is drawn as the mockup's white disc, navy ring and blue \"i\" (`CasualNoticeInfo`) instead of the old sprite. Measured on three boards: font size difference 0, text position within 3 px, toast box size equal. The toast's vertical place follows the live HUD top and was not measured. At the 1:1 editor board text is about 4% narrower because legacy `Text` rounds glyph advances to whole pixels; at device scale that falls below 1%.\n\n## Town plaza (partly applied)\n\nThe existing build already matched font sizes and most box positions. Fixed in this pass: the interaction card detail now starts on its first line (+10 px), the talk button carries the mockup's chat glyph as a centred row, the title plate's 3 px shadow is part of its rect, and the PC guide panel uses the 3 px border inset and 556 px height. Kept on purpose: the top-right cog, content dock and event button follow PR #144 (2026-10-18, \"shortcuts beneath a painted cog\") and the `menu-*` medallion art, so they differ from the mockup's flat cog and dock place. The shared HUD text sizes and the portrait slot row were matched under \"Shared HUD\" below. The place name contains a glyph outside the Jua subset (\"잿\"), so it falls back to Noto and is about 9 px wider. The card and name tags are enlarged by a transform scale (cardScale/scale), so on small canvases (0.54 on portrait) their text is rasterised at canvas resolution and magnified (may look soft, follow-up).\n\n## Daily quests and attendance (applied)\n\nThe three pages are redrawn on `EventsPlate` (the mockup's cream plate with masthead, three tabs and footer strip) and `ContentWindowView`'s fitted mode (`Open(..., fit)`, `RepaintFitted` draws only the scrim and one plate). Every size is the mockup's CSS pixels; `UiMock.WindowScale` (scale from the whole screen) and `UiMock.Plate` (safe area less 8 px, PC capped at 1500×810) place the plate. The old build stretched with the screen aspect. Final measurement: daily quests have 32/37 (portrait) and 37/43 (PC) texts at the mockup's size and place (the rest are ≤4% width differences and glyphs outside the Jua subset), 7-day attendance 21/21 on PC and 28-day 17/17. The tab waiting dots, footer strips, remaining time (HH:MM:SS) and claim toast follow the mockup. Because of the mockup's `.dq button{margin:0;font:inherit;color:inherit}`, the DOM draws tab and claim text as navy Noto 14 px and the title as gilt Jua, so the DOM dump was the reference. Remaining: the dump cannot read the shadow (UiWindowFrame +5 px) or clipped scroll content, and the daily claim/refresh toast and the closed states were not measured.\n\n## Shared HUD (applied)\n\n`GlobalHudLayout.HudFont` sets the level, HP, resource, EXP, caption and cooldown text to the mockup's per-board size (PC, landscape, portrait), and the portrait slot row baseline (147) and shortcut captions (below the slots) follow the mockup. Checked on plaza HUD captures: Lv 17/13/13, HP and resource 13/10/10, EXP and captions 15/11/11, cooldown numbers 26/15/17 (PC/portrait/landscape). The plaza and battle HUD mockups disagree by about 1 px on HUD text and on the portrait slot size (46 vs 42); the game follows the plaza mockup.\n\n## Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)\n\nThe legacy `HuntEdictWindow` is about to be removed, so work stopped there on the user's instruction. Applied: the mockup's border-box frame (padding inside the 3 px border, header, rule and tab spacing, a dashed footer rule with the buttons), drop shadows hanging outside the rect so control sizes equal the mockup boxes (`UiButtonFace.ShadowOutside`, `HuntEdictSurface.shadowOutside`), the header subtitle pill, tab icon and label placement, the overview switch row and style cards, the group editor (search row, group chips, white section card, quick-preset stack, option rows), the skill manage list, the equipped-skills dock, the policy page header, preset tabs and the rail dock. Measured: on PC the combat, loot, bag, repeat and explore (custom rows) tabs match almost every text in size and place, with 1-2 box deviations. What remains is the Noto fallback for strings with the middle dot (not in the Jua subset, 3 to 14 px wider) and `.5` font sizes rounding. Not done: presets, recommended equipment, dialogs and the survival popups (hazard canvas) - skipped because they are legacy. Found on the way: the window's palette fields read `Resources` in a `MonoBehaviour` field initializer, which made the first text transparent; they are now read on use.\n\n## Settings window (applied)\n\nThe settings canvas used `ApplyScaler(..., *.5)`, which drew it about 20% smaller than the mockup on phones; it now uses `UiMock.CanvasScale` (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel) and a per-board table `SettingsLook` (padding, gap, font, tab height, chip, button, bar, seal) for the frame (title, close, rule, tab row, scrolling body), card padding, section headers, body and muted copy (with line height, `SettingsParagraph`), notices, sliders (bar `barh`, 17 px marker, ticks), choice cards (map, language, hero), buttons and the UID strip. The 12 px body scrollbar takes width only when content overflows; the audio tab uses volume rows (no gap) and a mute card (checkbox and two lines); the character tab holds the hero cards in one card. Measured: PC screen tab 17 of 19 texts and language 11 of 12 match; audio, character and account have the structure and 7-9 texts at the same size and place. Remaining: the value `%` drawn apart from the number, the audio bar markers, account UID and pending counts (demo data), notice line counts (text width); the guide and combat-state tabs use the same table but were not measured separately. `CasualSettingsTests` now measure in mockup units (tab width and height, UID strip, slider touch area).\n\n## Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)\n\nThe mockup (`Equipment shop - casual`) differs from the old screen in structure, so the window was redrawn in the mockup's pixels instead of retuning values (`EquipmentShopWindow.Mock*.cs`; the gamble merchant and the auto-selection settings shared with salvage keep their old layout). One canvas unit is one mockup pixel and the PC board is 1440 wide, hence the new `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)`. Built new: the header (brand, place, title, close), the tab row with the restock countdown, rarity and order selects (small list popups) and the category strip, 56 px offer rows with the \"Small upgrade\" tag, the inventory grid (8 columns, 50 slots, categories, the multi-select bar, the auto-select notice band), two comparison popups for buy, sell and buyback (deltas, lost properties, upgrade record, fact table) and the dock under them, the footer (gold, gems, status, History, Help), thin scrollbars that take width only when content overflows, the dialog frame (sale confirmation, item details, presets, history, help) and the auto-selection settings (rarity chips, equipment kinds, details, exclusion rules). History is backed by `EquipmentShopState.ledger` (the last 30 purchases, sales and buybacks, newest first; an additive save field, old saves load an empty list). At the user's request the item picture in the comparison popups is larger than the mockup's 40 px (72 px on PC, 52 px on phones). Measured: on the PC buy, sell, multi-sale, auto-select settings and details boards texts and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px and control sizes match (grid cell 63 px, offer row 56 px, popup 425.5 px). Remaining: the middle dot missing from the Jua subset makes the title and place strings fall back to Noto (3 to 14 px wider) and Jua digits are about 10% wider, no \"Unique\" rarity chip (a reserved legacy bit that cannot be selected), the mockup's EN/KO toggle is left to the language setting, and the enlarged item picture (requested). 60 new strings carry English in `en.txt`.\n\n## Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)\n\nThe blacksmith mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Blacksmith - casual`) differs from the old screen in structure (free-floating round header buttons, five pill tabs, rounded panel cards, a docked action bar), so it was redrawn in the mockup's unit `U` (PC 14 px, phone landscape 12.32 px, phone portrait 13.86 px). Built new: the shared frame (header, tabs, wallets, lock tip, sealed screen, toast), option change (item list, option rows, selection dock), slot enhancement (slot rows, work status, workshop, growth table, unlock popup), gear enhancement (item list, growth steps, quote dock, auto-enhance settings and result, confirm popup), core crafting (core equipment panel, catalogue, forge, quality gauge, result and history popups), the item picker sheet and the help dialog. Files are split into `BlacksmithWindow.*Screen.cs`, `*Dialogs.cs`, `Modal.cs`, `Overlays.cs` and `CoresEquip.cs`; the capture driver is `UiFidelityForge.cs` (specs enchant/slots/gear/cores with `+confirm`, `+hit`, `+complete`, `+workshop`, `+unlock`, `+plan`, `+core-*`, `sealed`, `locktip`). Measured: on the PC, phone landscape and phone portrait boards text sizes match and text and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px; only a few knife-edge line breaks differ because Jua is about 10% wider. The mockup's demo-only history and reset popups were not built (the game has no reroll history). New strings carry English in `en.txt` and the old ones were pruned.\n\n## Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)\n\nThe jeweler mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Jeweler - casual`) differs from the old window (three tabs, gem list, potion rows), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel, `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)`). Its CSS answers the board size with container queries, so `JewelerLook` starts from the base rules and overrides them in the stylesheet's own order (wide >=1550, mid 681-1100, short <=600, mobile <=680). Built new: the masthead (gem emblem, gilt title, active elixir, tier values, sockets, close), the service bar, the 6x6 owned-gem matrix with the inspected gem and tier conversion (1, 5, all), six gem tabs and six elixir cards (three effects, owned, state), the docked brew bar (quantity minus/plus/max input and the brew button), the footer note, the toast and the tier values dialog. Game-side changes: `Jeweler.Craft(count)` and `CraftLimit` let the player brew any quantity (the `GemBatch` versions are unchanged); the elixir pictures use the game's approved elixir art in the same box instead of the mockup's SVG vials. The mockup's demo-only parts (the \"try elixir\" button, the reset dialog, the draft note) were not built; the close button, the sockets button and the broken-gem purchase row were added because the game needs them. `MockKit` is the new shared pixel helper (text boxes, cards, press, icons, thin scrolling) the next windows reuse. Measured (`UiFidelityJeweler.cs`: brew, convert, empty, crafted, balance, with `@inv|@pot|@body|@potions` scroll variants): on PC 1440x810 and 1440x900, phone landscape and phone portrait every text size matches and positions sit within 0-2 px (brew dock, matrix, cards and dialog included). Remaining: the description paragraph sits 1-2 px low, dashed outlines of empty cells, the game's elixir art instead of the SVG vials, wrap widths of the shield and long sentences (Unity breaks Hangul lines differently); the wide (>=1550) and short (601-760 tall) rules were transcribed from the CSS but could not be measured. New strings carry the mockup's English in `en.txt` (\"Jeweler (casual)\") and 21 old entries were pruned.\n\n## Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)\n\nThe rift entry mockup (`HELLSCRIPT Rift Entry - casual`) differs from the old window (title row, scrolling body, bottom buttons), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels. `RiftEntryWindow.Fit` uses `UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)` to make one canvas unit one mockup pixel (the boards are full-bleed, no safe area) and takes the mockup's `--b` (PC/landscape `clamp(9, min(1.4583%W, 2.9167%H), 24)`, portrait `min(3.5%W, 1.842%H)`) as the one unit every size is a multiple of. Wide boards use three columns (art .95/3 · fatigue, potions, skills · entry), portrait stacks them and shares the spare height as `(H-content)/4` gaps (at least .3B). New: the masthead (back, logo, divider, title, abyssal coins, close), the portal art panel with tier picker, record and first-clear chest, the fatigue gauge (daily and restored spans, reset time, carry-over), three equipped-potion rows, five skill tiles (four actives and the ultimate), the services row, three entry buttons (1x, 1.5x, resume) and the help button. Modals follow the mockup's `.modal` rules (wide, narrow <=650, short <=570) through `ModalLook` and are drawn into their own fixed views: 1.5x confirm, fatigue recovery confirm, fatigue help, potion picker (six elixir tabs plus an ordinary-potion tab, six tier cards), skill/ultimate picker (three-column option cards, details box, Close/Replace) and first-clear rewards (virtualized 1,000-tier list). Game rules and saves are unchanged (`Store.ReplaceRiftSkill`, `game.SelectRiftPotion` and the fatigue paths are the same) and the window keeps its old control names (`rift-enter-fast`, `rift-skill-N`, `rift-potion-N`, ...). The two portal paintings (tall and wide) were generated with Codex and installed under `Resources/Art/RiftEntry/Casual/` (record: `Docs/Art/RiftEntryPortal20261020/`, model not reported, the generator's own check flagged the composition, so not production-approved). Measurement (`UiFidelityRift.cs`: lobby, newtier, claimed, empty, mixed, limit, poor and the modals fast, recover, help, potion, potiondiamond, skill, ultimate, rewards): the three lobby boards and the confirm, recover and help modals match in text size with positions inside 0-1 px; the potion, skill, ultimate and rewards modals are within +-0.5 px in text size (legacy Text uses whole sizes) and 0-2 px in position. Deliberate differences from the mockup: the potion tabs gain an \"Ordinary potion\" tab the mockup lacks (ordinary potions stay equippable), the reward rows show the real reward chest art with claim state and unlock lines instead of the empty placeholder and the \"next content\" hint moved from the lobby to the top of that modal, and the skill details append rank, damage, cost and activation order (real data) under the mockup's one-line description, so the body scrolls on short boards. Titles containing a middle dot (.) are drawn in Noto because of the Jua glyph limit. 38 new strings were added to `en.txt` in the mockup's English and four changed ones (Replace skill, Enter Rift, Change potion, the tagline) were aligned to it. The native smokes (`RuntimeRiftEntrySmoke*` and friends) were adapted to the new structure (fixed view, modal names, virtual list) but not run this time.\n\n## Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)\n\nThe gear offer mockup (`Rift gear offer UI - casual`: battle cards, details, compare and settings) differs from the old windows (a header/tabs/scroll shell hosting the shared `ItemDetailView` and `EquipmentComparisonView`), so the detail/compare and settings windows were redrawn in mockup pixels. `RiftRecommendationWindow` uses `RiftEntryWindow.Fit` (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel; cards, rows and cells live in `RiftRecommendationWindow.Look.cs`) and draws the compare tab, the ring target choice (left/right), the worn and recommended cards, the score line (old -> new), the real stat rows (base, fixed resistance, affixes, special effects, set bonuses) and the pinned Equip/Close row. `RiftGearSettingsWindow` draws the numbered sections (I-IV), pill choices, per-position detail panels (threshold fields, add-option list), the time slider and the pinned Save/Close row as in the mockup; the landscape board is a 560x424 window whose body alone scrolls (the mockup shows it scrolled by 26 px, so it matches at the same offset). The battle cards (`GameUI.GearRecommendations`) follow the mockup card rules (inner 1 px border, radius 12, shadow outside): the card is 92x150, the score pill 80x17, the Equip button 58x20 and the gear/close buttons 24 px, all at the mockup positions. Game rules and transactions (`EquipInventoryItem`, `SaveRiftGearOfferSettings`) and the old control names are unchanged, and the real item and setting values replace the mockup's samples in the same row look (a longer real stat list makes the body scroll). Decorations only the mockup has (the Equip shine, blurred shadows) are not drawn. Measurement (`UiFidelityGear.cs`: portrait and landscape detail, compare, settings and the battle-card `Strip`): detail and compare match in title, tab and button text size with panel offsets of 0, settings match in text size within 0-1 px of position (landscape at the same 26 px scroll) and the card interior is within 0-1 px. Remaining differences: `+ Add condition option` is drawn in Noto because of its full-width plus, and the inset shadow bands and the button shine are missing. Nine new strings were added to `en.txt` in the mockup's English. The mockup's `Run` and `Closed` boards (the sanctuary hub during a run, a closed window) have no matching window and were not built.\n\n## Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)\n\nThe training boards (`training` canvas, 1600 px: lobby, add-enemy dialog, result faster/slower/first clear/failed) differ in structure from the old window (two columns, a hero picture and drop-down tier/count), so the lobby, the add-enemy dialog and the result were redrawn in the mockup pixels. `TrainingGroundWindow` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`TrainingGroundWindow.Look.cs` holds the text, spacing and big-number shrinking, `.Lobby.cs` the lobby body, `.Picker.cs` the add-enemy dialog) and draws the skill rows (checkbox, picture, edict-edit button), the boss/normal enemy rows (four tier buttons `training-tier-<key>-<tier>`, count minus/value/plus `training-count-<key>-minus|plus|value`), the rift tier slider and the start button as laid out in the mockup. `TrainingGroundResultWindow` is three columns (PC), two (landscape phone) or one scrolling column over a pinned footer (portrait phone), and draws the clear time, the comparison box against the previous run (faster/slower/same/first record/failed copy), the DPS chart (this run and the previous one, cast marks in `DPS chart`), the damage-by-skill rows (normal/boss bars, uses, edict edit), the changed Hunt Edict list and the change-setup/restart footer. Game rules and records (`TrainingGround.*`, `RecordTrainingGround`) and most control names are unchanged; the drop-downs `training-tier-<key>`/`training-count-<key>` are now group objects with the buttons named above (`RuntimeTrainingGroundSmoke` updated). On the result, `victory-skill-N`, `Skill uses` and `Result summary` are gone and `training-back`, `training-result-skill-<id>` and `training-result-basic` are new. The phone boards carry a sample notch and home bar, so the driver (`UiFidelityTraining.cs`) simulates the same insets through `UiSafeArea.Simulate`. Measured: lobby and dialog texts are within 0 to 0.5 px of font size and 0 to 3 px of position (apart from rows that differ between the mockup sample data and real data); the three result states match in title, time, box, chart and row font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px, with vertical positions following the row count. The 20 new strings are filled in `en.txt` with the mockup English (10K/100M/T are only used by a Korean-only branch) and 8 unused old strings were removed. Remaining difference: the big time digits and the logo letter-spacing are slightly narrower in the Jua subset font.\n\n## Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)\n\nThe codex boards (`codex` canvas: 956 px landscape and 440 px portrait, list, normal detail, boss detail) are also drawn at 956 px wide on PC, so `MonsterCodexWindow` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`UiMock.WindowScale(safe,956)`). `MonsterCodexWindow.Look.cs` holds the text line boxes, the sky, the masthead (back button `codex-back`, logo, gilt title, `codex-crumb` in the detail), the progress bar `codex-progress`, the list (8 landscape / 4 portrait columns of `codex-card-<id>` cards with portrait frame, ID chip, boss ribbon and kill count) and the horizontally scrolling field tabs `codex-tab-<f>`; `MonsterCodexWindow.Detail.cs` holds the detail (identity box, life/attack/speed `codex-life|attack|speed` with notes, damage types, defence cells `codex-defense-<i>`, the skills panel `codex-skills` with rows, tag chips and boss skill icons, the rift tier bar `codex-tier-*` with slider, best-clear marker and the Normal/Elite `codex-grade` control or the boss tier chip). The portrait detail scrolls as one page. Game rules (`MonsterCodex.*`, tier and elite preview) and most control names are unchanged and the old `MonsterCodexWindow.Skin.cs` is gone. The chip and rule colours were added as `UiTheme.MockChipWash/MockChipInk/MockRowRule`. The driver `UiFidelityCodex.cs` captures the list, the 8th normal monster and the first boss at the same screen sizes with an account that has kill counts. Measured: font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px and positions within 0 to 3 px (apart from mockup sample versus real monster/skill text lengths and field tab widths). The 7 new strings are filled in `en.txt`. Remaining differences: logo letter-spacing width, tab height 36 px (below the 44 px touch rule, as in the mockup) and integer font-size rounding of 0.5 px. The native `RuntimeMonsterCodexSmoke` was adapted but not run.\n\n## Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)\n\nThe aspect stone boards (`aspect` canvas, 32 boards: imprint, growth, max level, uncollected, not eligible, empty search, already imprinted, replace/new confirmation, completion, guide, gear picker, the three portrait steps) differ in structure from the old window (one card list and an imprint button below), so the whole window was redrawn in the mockup pixels. `AspectStoneSession` fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through `Fit` (`UiMock.WindowScale(safe,1440)`); the board sizes live in `AspectLook` (PC, landscape and portrait columns), the masthead, service bar, phone step strip and docked action bar in `AspectStoneWindow.Chrome.cs`, the three-panel workbench (worn gear, aspect codex, imprint preview) in `.Panels.cs` and `.Detail.cs`, the popups in `.Dialogs.cs`. Worn gear is ten slots (`aspect-slot-<slot>-<index>`) around the character figure; the codex has the search box, the class drop-down (`aspect-class`), four state tabs (`aspect-filter-0..3`: all, collected, uncollected, upgrade) and 5/4/3 columns of cards (`aspect-select-<id>`); the preview draws the five level boxes, the level-up button (`aspect-upgrade`), the source, the current/new aspect comparison (`aspect-compare`) and the notes. The imprint confirmation and its completion stay separate windows on the shared host (`aspect-confirm-commit/cancel`, `aspect-success-close`); the portrait phone uses the step strip (`aspect-step-1..3`) and the gear picker (`aspect-equipment-picker`, `aspect-equip-<id>`). Game rules (`AspectStone.*`, `UpgradeAspect`, `ImprintAspect`) are unchanged. The mockup's \"In the field\" tab, demo footer and salvage demo modal are features the game does not have and are not drawn; the mockup has no close control, so `content-close` was added at the end of the service bar. The driver `UiFidelityAspect.cs` captures imprint, growth, low, max, locked, ineligible, empty, already, replace, confirm, success, help, picker, gear and detail at the same screen sizes with a nine-piece gear set and codex data (landscape uses `Simulate(53,27,53,8)`, portrait `Simulate(0,26,0,52)`). Measured: PC and portrait font sizes match exactly and positions are within 0 to 3 px (apart from row counts and font weight); landscape masthead, service bar and detail panel are within 0 to 1 px. The 48 new strings are filled in `en.txt` with the mockup English (the guide and part of the confirmation have no English board, so they were translated by hand). Remaining differences: the mockup's portrait step labels collapse into two squeezed lines that overlap the number disc (a CSS bug), so they are drawn as one centred line; the mockup's narrow landscape English folds the \"Equipped gear\" header onto two lines and pushes everything below by 17 px, which is not reproduced; the runestone SVG is replaced by the game's existing rune art. The native `RuntimeAspectStoneSmoke` was adapted but not run.\n\n## Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)\n\nCharacter creation and selection (`Docs/Design/CharacterPlaza`, 28 boards) is a full-screen scene with painted heroes, not a content window. `CharacterSelectionView` is already built from the mockup's `layout()` numbers (design unit = mockup px through `PixelScale`), so the back button, heading, play button, crests, name tag and badge match. Reading the code against `layout()` line by line, only the differing values were changed: the hint pill (landscape width 320 and height 22, PC height 22, portrait 26, landscape type 12.5), the landscape badge right limit (W−112), the play type size (landscape 22), and the gear popup (panel top 150/90/58, grid side 18/21/22, gap 12/14/12, top 96/100/84, cell height 118/150/112, icon 52/60/48, name and slot type sizes, title and hero line positions, close position, a vertically centred stack inside each cell). The hero placement and animation on the scene were not touched. The screen only exists inside a signed-in `GameUI` session, so no editor capture driver was written, and `RuntimeCharacterSelectionSmoke` was not run; the visual check of this screen is still open.\n\n## Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)\n\nThe rune board boards (`runeboard` canvas, 32 boards) are a new visual system: a light hex board with coloured blocks, six weapon cards, a mastery strip, storage and selection panels, and the practice, guide, preset and region dialogs, while the game's `RuneBoardWindow` uses the dark stone board art and `U`-unit layout. `UiFidelityRuneBoard.cs` captured the PC, landscape and portrait baselines: its type is 1.3 to 1.4 times the mockup's and the board art itself differs, so this is not a handful of numbers but a separate job that rebuilds the board drawing (`RuneBoardGraphic`) and every dialog. It risks the rune editing, tutorial and practice flows, so it was not redrawn in this pass.\n\n## Not audited yet\n\nBattle HUD graphs and observation menu (part of the whole battle HUD), redrawing the rune board (see the baseline above), and a visual check of the character selection screen. Download each canvas from Claude Design (procedure in `tools/ui_fidelity/README.md`) and measure it the same way.\n\n## Verification\n\nShared HUD, plaza and battle HUD: focused EditMode 112 tests (GlobalHudTests, CasualHudSharedTests, SharedUiTests, TownPlazaLayoutTests, TownWalkTests, CasualBattleHudTests, GlobalHudResourceTests, HudInspectionCasualTests) passed, daily quests and attendance: focused EditMode (DailyQuestSkinTests, AttendanceArtTests, AttendanceTests, LocalizationTests: 66 tests) passed, shared notices: 16 tests (CasualNoticeLayoutTests), town plaza layout: 19 tests (TownPlazaLayoutTests), rune master: focused EditMode 8 tests (RuneMasterCasualSkinTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed. Warehouse: focused EditMode 89 tests (StorageCasualSkinTests, StorageTests, UiButtonTests, EquipmentStorageTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed. Rift entry: focused EditMode 66 tests (RiftEntryCasualWindowTests, RiftEntryTests, LocalizationTests, CasualImportedAssetTests) passed, `check_ui_contract.py --verify` 15 tests passed, the native smokes were adapted but not run. Full EditMode, macOS player, devices and performance were not run.\n\n## Next\n\nAudit the windows under \"Not audited yet\" the same way (each needs an Editor driver `UiFidelity*.cs`; windows tied to GameUI are driven like `UiFidelityPlaza`, filling the fields without a `GameController` run). The skill tree already has a golden check with zero position and size difference.\n\n## Integration review correction on 2026-10-21\n\nThe official review found missing special effects, sockets and ring/dual-wield replacement selection in the new shop cards. The detail panel and bulk-sale item dialog now use the shared `EquipmentComparisonView`, restoring the complete tooltip, replacement targets and option ranges while retaining the transaction dock. The earlier shop-card pixel measurements do not validate this shared comparison area.\n", "html": "<p><a href=\"#/page/casual-ui-fidelity-audit-20261020\">한국어<\/a><\/p><h2 id=\"method\">Method<\/h2><p>Every board of a mockup canvas (Claude Design) is rendered on a local server and every text, box and image rect is extracted in board pixels (<code>tools/ui_fidelity/measure.html<\/code>). The same values are extracted from the real window at the same size (a floating Unity Game View resized to 440×956 / 956×440 / 1600×900, <code>UiFidelityDump<\/code>) and compared with <code>compare.py<\/code> (text matched by string, boxes by IoU). Boards: portrait 440×956, phone landscape 956×440, PC 1600×900.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-cause-and-shared-fix\">Shared cause and shared fix<\/h2><ul><li>Earlier windows used game units (<code>UiTheme.Scale<\/code>) with ad-hoc constants that followed the mockup &quot;in proportion&quot; only: on the same board text was about 2 px small and cells, tabs and gaps differed.<\/li><li><code>UiMock<\/code> (board class + <code>UiTheme.Scale / reference scale<\/code>) makes one canvas unit equal one mockup pixel on the reference board and scales the board proportionally elsewhere. A window uses the mockup CSS tokens (<code>--cell<\/code>, <code>--head<\/code>, <code>--fs<\/code> …) directly.<\/li><li><code>StorageSurface.shadowOutside<\/code> and an extended button chrome hang the box-shadow outside the box, so the layout rect equals the mockup border-box.<\/li><li><code>EquipmentSlotView.Paint(…, CellRecipe)<\/code> draws the inner slot layout (art 86% at 7%/5%, level .17, chips .14/.2/.22) proportionally; other windows keep their look without a recipe.<\/li><\/ul><h2 id=\"warehouse-applied\">Warehouse (applied)<\/h2><p>Measured against the old window (portrait): title 22.8→25, tab numerals 8.7→11, category 10.9→13, footer currency 11.9→16 px, cell 56.5→52, vault tab 38→46, plus category row, well, rail and footer positions now follow the mockup. Final measurement: normal and bulk states on all three boards are within 0.5 px in font size (integer font rounding) and mostly within 2 px in box position and size; only the PC tool chips and the usage pill are off by up to about 5–9 px. Dialogs match in width and font size; the expand dialog matches the 382 px frame height, help and open-tab are within ±5 px. The grade popover matches in position and was +14 px tall; that was corrected but not re-measured.<\/p><p>Known remaining gaps: drag ghost, pause pill and toast boards are not measured yet. Detail and preset dialog internals belong to shared parts (<code>ItemDetailView<\/code>, <code>CharacterEquipmentView<\/code>). The brand letter spacing is drawn by <code>UiTextSpacing<\/code> but the dump reads glyph width before the effect; the usage pill width differs from the mockup sample (&quot;12 / 60&quot;) with the count string.<\/p><h2 id=\"rune-master-applied\">Rune master (applied)<\/h2><p><code>RuneMasterWindow.Layout.cs<\/code> draws the reshape and ascend tabs in the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels (per-board head, footer, chips, slots, material cells and the fuse button). Final measurement: the three reshape boards and the PC idle/pair/error states differ in only 1–2 of 12–13 texts (the brand letter-spacing width and a 4% narrower long note on the landscape board), and portrait box deviation is zero. Real differences fixed in this pass: footer skip-button height (landscape +4, portrait +2), landscape ascend group row height 51→53, the ascend summary line height now uses the mockup&#x27;s fractional size instead of the rounded font, two-digit grade chip counts wrapping on portrait, and the initial scrollbar position. Remaining: the bottom empty bag cells on PC/landscape look shorter in the dump only because the viewport clips them (raw height 91.6 matches); the usage pill differs by about 3 px with digit width; the ascend board is driven with a different group state than each mockup board (each board registers a different cell count), so the registered-materials row is matched by formula (material and result cell heights) only.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-confirm-and-toast-applied\">Shared confirm and toast (applied)<\/h2><p><code>CasualNoticeLayout<\/code> now computes the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels times <code>UiMock.CanvasScale<\/code> in physical pixels (<code>For(safe, scale)<\/code>, the device value is <code>Current()<\/code>); before, it used raw <code>Screen<\/code> pixels and drew at about a third of the mockup on a DPR 3 phone. The card is border-box like the mockup (the border sits outside the padding); the toast groups portrait with landscape for border, shadow, padding and the 9 px gap (<code>.o-land .tst,.o-port .tst<\/code>), the info badge and text are one centred group, and the badge is drawn as the mockup&#x27;s white disc, navy ring and blue &quot;i&quot; (<code>CasualNoticeInfo<\/code>) instead of the old sprite. Measured on three boards: font size difference 0, text position within 3 px, toast box size equal. The toast&#x27;s vertical place follows the live HUD top and was not measured. At the 1:1 editor board text is about 4% narrower because legacy <code>Text<\/code> rounds glyph advances to whole pixels; at device scale that falls below 1%.<\/p><h2 id=\"town-plaza-partly-applied\">Town plaza (partly applied)<\/h2><p>The existing build already matched font sizes and most box positions. Fixed in this pass: the interaction card detail now starts on its first line (+10 px), the talk button carries the mockup&#x27;s chat glyph as a centred row, the title plate&#x27;s 3 px shadow is part of its rect, and the PC guide panel uses the 3 px border inset and 556 px height. Kept on purpose: the top-right cog, content dock and event button follow PR #144 (2026-10-18, &quot;shortcuts beneath a painted cog&quot;) and the <code>menu-*<\/code> medallion art, so they differ from the mockup&#x27;s flat cog and dock place. The shared HUD text sizes and the portrait slot row were matched under &quot;Shared HUD&quot; below. The place name contains a glyph outside the Jua subset (&quot;잿&quot;), so it falls back to Noto and is about 9 px wider. The card and name tags are enlarged by a transform scale (cardScale/scale), so on small canvases (0.54 on portrait) their text is rasterised at canvas resolution and magnified (may look soft, follow-up).<\/p><h2 id=\"daily-quests-and-attendance-applied\">Daily quests and attendance (applied)<\/h2><p>The three pages are redrawn on <code>EventsPlate<\/code> (the mockup&#x27;s cream plate with masthead, three tabs and footer strip) and <code>ContentWindowView<\/code>&#x27;s fitted mode (<code>Open(..., fit)<\/code>, <code>RepaintFitted<\/code> draws only the scrim and one plate). Every size is the mockup&#x27;s CSS pixels; <code>UiMock.WindowScale<\/code> (scale from the whole screen) and <code>UiMock.Plate<\/code> (safe area less 8 px, PC capped at 1500×810) place the plate. The old build stretched with the screen aspect. Final measurement: daily quests have 32/37 (portrait) and 37/43 (PC) texts at the mockup&#x27;s size and place (the rest are ≤4% width differences and glyphs outside the Jua subset), 7-day attendance 21/21 on PC and 28-day 17/17. The tab waiting dots, footer strips, remaining time (HH:MM:SS) and claim toast follow the mockup. Because of the mockup&#x27;s <code>.dq button{margin:0;font:inherit;color:inherit}<\/code>, the DOM draws tab and claim text as navy Noto 14 px and the title as gilt Jua, so the DOM dump was the reference. Remaining: the dump cannot read the shadow (UiWindowFrame +5 px) or clipped scroll content, and the daily claim/refresh toast and the closed states were not measured.<\/p><h2 id=\"shared-hud-applied\">Shared HUD (applied)<\/h2><p><code>GlobalHudLayout.HudFont<\/code> sets the level, HP, resource, EXP, caption and cooldown text to the mockup&#x27;s per-board size (PC, landscape, portrait), and the portrait slot row baseline (147) and shortcut captions (below the slots) follow the mockup. Checked on plaza HUD captures: Lv 17/13/13, HP and resource 13/10/10, EXP and captions 15/11/11, cooldown numbers 26/15/17 (PC/portrait/landscape). The plaza and battle HUD mockups disagree by about 1 px on HUD text and on the portrait slot size (46 vs 42); the game follows the plaza mockup.<\/p><h2 id=\"hunt-edict-window-legacy-partly-applied\">Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)<\/h2><p>The legacy <code>HuntEdictWindow<\/code> is about to be removed, so work stopped there on the user&#x27;s instruction. Applied: the mockup&#x27;s border-box frame (padding inside the 3 px border, header, rule and tab spacing, a dashed footer rule with the buttons), drop shadows hanging outside the rect so control sizes equal the mockup boxes (<code>UiButtonFace.ShadowOutside<\/code>, <code>HuntEdictSurface.shadowOutside<\/code>), the header subtitle pill, tab icon and label placement, the overview switch row and style cards, the group editor (search row, group chips, white section card, quick-preset stack, option rows), the skill manage list, the equipped-skills dock, the policy page header, preset tabs and the rail dock. Measured: on PC the combat, loot, bag, repeat and explore (custom rows) tabs match almost every text in size and place, with 1-2 box deviations. What remains is the Noto fallback for strings with the middle dot (not in the Jua subset, 3 to 14 px wider) and <code>.5<\/code> font sizes rounding. Not done: presets, recommended equipment, dialogs and the survival popups (hazard canvas) - skipped because they are legacy. Found on the way: the window&#x27;s palette fields read <code>Resources<\/code> in a <code>MonoBehaviour<\/code> field initializer, which made the first text transparent; they are now read on use.<\/p><h2 id=\"settings-window-applied\">Settings window (applied)<\/h2><p>The settings canvas used <code>ApplyScaler(..., *.5)<\/code>, which drew it about 20% smaller than the mockup on phones; it now uses <code>UiMock.CanvasScale<\/code> (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel) and a per-board table <code>SettingsLook<\/code> (padding, gap, font, tab height, chip, button, bar, seal) for the frame (title, close, rule, tab row, scrolling body), card padding, section headers, body and muted copy (with line height, <code>SettingsParagraph<\/code>), notices, sliders (bar <code>barh<\/code>, 17 px marker, ticks), choice cards (map, language, hero), buttons and the UID strip. The 12 px body scrollbar takes width only when content overflows; the audio tab uses volume rows (no gap) and a mute card (checkbox and two lines); the character tab holds the hero cards in one card. Measured: PC screen tab 17 of 19 texts and language 11 of 12 match; audio, character and account have the structure and 7-9 texts at the same size and place. Remaining: the value <code>%<\/code> drawn apart from the number, the audio bar markers, account UID and pending counts (demo data), notice line counts (text width); the guide and combat-state tabs use the same table but were not measured separately. <code>CasualSettingsTests<\/code> now measure in mockup units (tab width and height, UID strip, slider touch area).<\/p><h2 id=\"equipment-shop-applied-pc-board-1440x810\">Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)<\/h2><p>The mockup (<code>Equipment shop - casual<\/code>) differs from the old screen in structure, so the window was redrawn in the mockup&#x27;s pixels instead of retuning values (<code>EquipmentShopWindow.Mock*.cs<\/code>; the gamble merchant and the auto-selection settings shared with salvage keep their old layout). One canvas unit is one mockup pixel and the PC board is 1440 wide, hence the new <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code>. Built new: the header (brand, place, title, close), the tab row with the restock countdown, rarity and order selects (small list popups) and the category strip, 56 px offer rows with the &quot;Small upgrade&quot; tag, the inventory grid (8 columns, 50 slots, categories, the multi-select bar, the auto-select notice band), two comparison popups for buy, sell and buyback (deltas, lost properties, upgrade record, fact table) and the dock under them, the footer (gold, gems, status, History, Help), thin scrollbars that take width only when content overflows, the dialog frame (sale confirmation, item details, presets, history, help) and the auto-selection settings (rarity chips, equipment kinds, details, exclusion rules). History is backed by <code>EquipmentShopState.ledger<\/code> (the last 30 purchases, sales and buybacks, newest first; an additive save field, old saves load an empty list). At the user&#x27;s request the item picture in the comparison popups is larger than the mockup&#x27;s 40 px (72 px on PC, 52 px on phones). Measured: on the PC buy, sell, multi-sale, auto-select settings and details boards texts and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px and control sizes match (grid cell 63 px, offer row 56 px, popup 425.5 px). Remaining: the middle dot missing from the Jua subset makes the title and place strings fall back to Noto (3 to 14 px wider) and Jua digits are about 10% wider, no &quot;Unique&quot; rarity chip (a reserved legacy bit that cannot be selected), the mockup&#x27;s EN/KO toggle is left to the language setting, and the enlarged item picture (requested). 60 new strings carry English in <code>en.txt<\/code>.<\/p><h2 id=\"blacksmith-applied-pc-board-1440x810\">Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)<\/h2><p>The blacksmith mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Blacksmith - casual<\/code>) differs from the old screen in structure (free-floating round header buttons, five pill tabs, rounded panel cards, a docked action bar), so it was redrawn in the mockup&#x27;s unit <code>U<\/code> (PC 14 px, phone landscape 12.32 px, phone portrait 13.86 px). Built new: the shared frame (header, tabs, wallets, lock tip, sealed screen, toast), option change (item list, option rows, selection dock), slot enhancement (slot rows, work status, workshop, growth table, unlock popup), gear enhancement (item list, growth steps, quote dock, auto-enhance settings and result, confirm popup), core crafting (core equipment panel, catalogue, forge, quality gauge, result and history popups), the item picker sheet and the help dialog. Files are split into <code>BlacksmithWindow.<em>Screen.cs<\/code>, <code><\/em>Dialogs.cs<\/code>, <code>Modal.cs<\/code>, <code>Overlays.cs<\/code> and <code>CoresEquip.cs<\/code>; the capture driver is <code>UiFidelityForge.cs<\/code> (specs enchant/slots/gear/cores with <code>+confirm<\/code>, <code>+hit<\/code>, <code>+complete<\/code>, <code>+workshop<\/code>, <code>+unlock<\/code>, <code>+plan<\/code>, <code>+core-*<\/code>, <code>sealed<\/code>, <code>locktip<\/code>). Measured: on the PC, phone landscape and phone portrait boards text sizes match and text and boxes sit within 0-1.5 px; only a few knife-edge line breaks differ because Jua is about 10% wider. The mockup&#x27;s demo-only history and reset popups were not built (the game has no reroll history). New strings carry English in <code>en.txt<\/code> and the old ones were pruned.<\/p><h2 id=\"jeweler-applied-pc-and-phone-boards\">Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)<\/h2><p>The jeweler mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Jeweler - casual<\/code>) differs from the old window (three tabs, gem list, potion rows), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel, <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code>). Its CSS answers the board size with container queries, so <code>JewelerLook<\/code> starts from the base rules and overrides them in the stylesheet&#x27;s own order (wide &gt;=1550, mid 681-1100, short &lt;=600, mobile &lt;=680). Built new: the masthead (gem emblem, gilt title, active elixir, tier values, sockets, close), the service bar, the 6x6 owned-gem matrix with the inspected gem and tier conversion (1, 5, all), six gem tabs and six elixir cards (three effects, owned, state), the docked brew bar (quantity minus/plus/max input and the brew button), the footer note, the toast and the tier values dialog. Game-side changes: <code>Jeweler.Craft(count)<\/code> and <code>CraftLimit<\/code> let the player brew any quantity (the <code>GemBatch<\/code> versions are unchanged); the elixir pictures use the game&#x27;s approved elixir art in the same box instead of the mockup&#x27;s SVG vials. The mockup&#x27;s demo-only parts (the &quot;try elixir&quot; button, the reset dialog, the draft note) were not built; the close button, the sockets button and the broken-gem purchase row were added because the game needs them. <code>MockKit<\/code> is the new shared pixel helper (text boxes, cards, press, icons, thin scrolling) the next windows reuse. Measured (<code>UiFidelityJeweler.cs<\/code>: brew, convert, empty, crafted, balance, with <code>@inv|@pot|@body|@potions<\/code> scroll variants): on PC 1440x810 and 1440x900, phone landscape and phone portrait every text size matches and positions sit within 0-2 px (brew dock, matrix, cards and dialog included). Remaining: the description paragraph sits 1-2 px low, dashed outlines of empty cells, the game&#x27;s elixir art instead of the SVG vials, wrap widths of the shield and long sentences (Unity breaks Hangul lines differently); the wide (&gt;=1550) and short (601-760 tall) rules were transcribed from the CSS but could not be measured. New strings carry the mockup&#x27;s English in <code>en.txt<\/code> (&quot;Jeweler (casual)&quot;) and 21 old entries were pruned.<\/p><h2 id=\"rift-entry-applied-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards-plus-eight-modals\">Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)<\/h2><p>The rift entry mockup (<code>HELLSCRIPT Rift Entry - casual<\/code>) differs from the old window (title row, scrolling body, bottom buttons), so it was redrawn in mockup pixels. <code>RiftEntryWindow.Fit<\/code> uses <code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe, 1440)<\/code> to make one canvas unit one mockup pixel (the boards are full-bleed, no safe area) and takes the mockup&#x27;s <code>--b<\/code> (PC/landscape <code>clamp(9, min(1.4583%W, 2.9167%H), 24)<\/code>, portrait <code>min(3.5%W, 1.842%H)<\/code>) as the one unit every size is a multiple of. Wide boards use three columns (art .95/3 · fatigue, potions, skills · entry), portrait stacks them and shares the spare height as <code>(H-content)/4<\/code> gaps (at least .3B). New: the masthead (back, logo, divider, title, abyssal coins, close), the portal art panel with tier picker, record and first-clear chest, the fatigue gauge (daily and restored spans, reset time, carry-over), three equipped-potion rows, five skill tiles (four actives and the ultimate), the services row, three entry buttons (1x, 1.5x, resume) and the help button. Modals follow the mockup&#x27;s <code>.modal<\/code> rules (wide, narrow &lt;=650, short &lt;=570) through <code>ModalLook<\/code> and are drawn into their own fixed views: 1.5x confirm, fatigue recovery confirm, fatigue help, potion picker (six elixir tabs plus an ordinary-potion tab, six tier cards), skill/ultimate picker (three-column option cards, details box, Close/Replace) and first-clear rewards (virtualized 1,000-tier list). Game rules and saves are unchanged (<code>Store.ReplaceRiftSkill<\/code>, <code>game.SelectRiftPotion<\/code> and the fatigue paths are the same) and the window keeps its old control names (<code>rift-enter-fast<\/code>, <code>rift-skill-N<\/code>, <code>rift-potion-N<\/code>, ...). The two portal paintings (tall and wide) were generated with Codex and installed under <code>Resources/Art/RiftEntry/Casual/<\/code> (record: <code>Docs/Art/RiftEntryPortal20261020/<\/code>, model not reported, the generator&#x27;s own check flagged the composition, so not production-approved). Measurement (<code>UiFidelityRift.cs<\/code>: lobby, newtier, claimed, empty, mixed, limit, poor and the modals fast, recover, help, potion, potiondiamond, skill, ultimate, rewards): the three lobby boards and the confirm, recover and help modals match in text size with positions inside 0-1 px; the potion, skill, ultimate and rewards modals are within +-0.5 px in text size (legacy Text uses whole sizes) and 0-2 px in position. Deliberate differences from the mockup: the potion tabs gain an &quot;Ordinary potion&quot; tab the mockup lacks (ordinary potions stay equippable), the reward rows show the real reward chest art with claim state and unlock lines instead of the empty placeholder and the &quot;next content&quot; hint moved from the lobby to the top of that modal, and the skill details append rank, damage, cost and activation order (real data) under the mockup&#x27;s one-line description, so the body scrolls on short boards. Titles containing a middle dot (.) are drawn in Noto because of the Jua glyph limit. 38 new strings were added to <code>en.txt<\/code> in the mockup&#x27;s English and four changed ones (Replace skill, Enter Rift, Change potion, the tagline) were aligned to it. The native smokes (<code>RuntimeRiftEntrySmoke*<\/code> and friends) were adapted to the new structure (fixed view, modal names, virtual list) but not run this time.<\/p><h2 id=\"rift-gear-offer-applied-portrait-440x956-and-landscape-956x440-boards\">Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)<\/h2><p>The gear offer mockup (<code>Rift gear offer UI - casual<\/code>: battle cards, details, compare and settings) differs from the old windows (a header/tabs/scroll shell hosting the shared <code>ItemDetailView<\/code> and <code>EquipmentComparisonView<\/code>), so the detail/compare and settings windows were redrawn in mockup pixels. <code>RiftRecommendationWindow<\/code> uses <code>RiftEntryWindow.Fit<\/code> (one canvas unit is one mockup pixel; cards, rows and cells live in <code>RiftRecommendationWindow.Look.cs<\/code>) and draws the compare tab, the ring target choice (left/right), the worn and recommended cards, the score line (old -&gt; new), the real stat rows (base, fixed resistance, affixes, special effects, set bonuses) and the pinned Equip/Close row. <code>RiftGearSettingsWindow<\/code> draws the numbered sections (I-IV), pill choices, per-position detail panels (threshold fields, add-option list), the time slider and the pinned Save/Close row as in the mockup; the landscape board is a 560x424 window whose body alone scrolls (the mockup shows it scrolled by 26 px, so it matches at the same offset). The battle cards (<code>GameUI.GearRecommendations<\/code>) follow the mockup card rules (inner 1 px border, radius 12, shadow outside): the card is 92x150, the score pill 80x17, the Equip button 58x20 and the gear/close buttons 24 px, all at the mockup positions. Game rules and transactions (<code>EquipInventoryItem<\/code>, <code>SaveRiftGearOfferSettings<\/code>) and the old control names are unchanged, and the real item and setting values replace the mockup&#x27;s samples in the same row look (a longer real stat list makes the body scroll). Decorations only the mockup has (the Equip shine, blurred shadows) are not drawn. Measurement (<code>UiFidelityGear.cs<\/code>: portrait and landscape detail, compare, settings and the battle-card <code>Strip<\/code>): detail and compare match in title, tab and button text size with panel offsets of 0, settings match in text size within 0-1 px of position (landscape at the same 26 px scroll) and the card interior is within 0-1 px. Remaining differences: <code>+ Add condition option<\/code> is drawn in Noto because of its full-width plus, and the inset shadow bands and the button shine are missing. Nine new strings were added to <code>en.txt<\/code> in the mockup&#x27;s English. The mockup&#x27;s <code>Run<\/code> and <code>Closed<\/code> boards (the sanctuary hub during a run, a closed window) have no matching window and were not built.<\/p><h2 id=\"training-ground-applied-lobby-add-enemy-dialog-and-three-result-states-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards\">Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)<\/h2><p>The training boards (<code>training<\/code> canvas, 1600 px: lobby, add-enemy dialog, result faster/slower/first clear/failed) differ in structure from the old window (two columns, a hero picture and drop-down tier/count), so the lobby, the add-enemy dialog and the result were redrawn in the mockup pixels. <code>TrainingGroundWindow<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>TrainingGroundWindow.Look.cs<\/code> holds the text, spacing and big-number shrinking, <code>.Lobby.cs<\/code> the lobby body, <code>.Picker.cs<\/code> the add-enemy dialog) and draws the skill rows (checkbox, picture, edict-edit button), the boss/normal enemy rows (four tier buttons <code>training-tier-&lt;key&gt;-&lt;tier&gt;<\/code>, count minus/value/plus <code>training-count-&lt;key&gt;-minus|plus|value<\/code>), the rift tier slider and the start button as laid out in the mockup. <code>TrainingGroundResultWindow<\/code> is three columns (PC), two (landscape phone) or one scrolling column over a pinned footer (portrait phone), and draws the clear time, the comparison box against the previous run (faster/slower/same/first record/failed copy), the DPS chart (this run and the previous one, cast marks in <code>DPS chart<\/code>), the damage-by-skill rows (normal/boss bars, uses, edict edit), the changed Hunt Edict list and the change-setup/restart footer. Game rules and records (<code>TrainingGround.*<\/code>, <code>RecordTrainingGround<\/code>) and most control names are unchanged; the drop-downs <code>training-tier-&lt;key&gt;<\/code>/<code>training-count-&lt;key&gt;<\/code> are now group objects with the buttons named above (<code>RuntimeTrainingGroundSmoke<\/code> updated). On the result, <code>victory-skill-N<\/code>, <code>Skill uses<\/code> and <code>Result summary<\/code> are gone and <code>training-back<\/code>, <code>training-result-skill-&lt;id&gt;<\/code> and <code>training-result-basic<\/code> are new. The phone boards carry a sample notch and home bar, so the driver (<code>UiFidelityTraining.cs<\/code>) simulates the same insets through <code>UiSafeArea.Simulate<\/code>. Measured: lobby and dialog texts are within 0 to 0.5 px of font size and 0 to 3 px of position (apart from rows that differ between the mockup sample data and real data); the three result states match in title, time, box, chart and row font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px, with vertical positions following the row count. The 20 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code> with the mockup English (10K/100M/T are only used by a Korean-only branch) and 8 unused old strings were removed. Remaining difference: the big time digits and the logo letter-spacing are slightly narrower in the Jua subset font.<\/p><h2 id=\"monster-codex-applied-list-normal-detail-and-boss-detail-landscape-and-portrait-boards\">Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)<\/h2><p>The codex boards (<code>codex<\/code> canvas: 956 px landscape and 440 px portrait, list, normal detail, boss detail) are also drawn at 956 px wide on PC, so <code>MonsterCodexWindow<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe,956)<\/code>). <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Look.cs<\/code> holds the text line boxes, the sky, the masthead (back button <code>codex-back<\/code>, logo, gilt title, <code>codex-crumb<\/code> in the detail), the progress bar <code>codex-progress<\/code>, the list (8 landscape / 4 portrait columns of <code>codex-card-&lt;id&gt;<\/code> cards with portrait frame, ID chip, boss ribbon and kill count) and the horizontally scrolling field tabs <code>codex-tab-&lt;f&gt;<\/code>; <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Detail.cs<\/code> holds the detail (identity box, life/attack/speed <code>codex-life|attack|speed<\/code> with notes, damage types, defence cells <code>codex-defense-&lt;i&gt;<\/code>, the skills panel <code>codex-skills<\/code> with rows, tag chips and boss skill icons, the rift tier bar <code>codex-tier-<em><\/code> with slider, best-clear marker and the Normal/Elite <code>codex-grade<\/code> control or the boss tier chip). The portrait detail scrolls as one page. Game rules (<code>MonsterCodex.<\/em><\/code>, tier and elite preview) and most control names are unchanged and the old <code>MonsterCodexWindow.Skin.cs<\/code> is gone. The chip and rule colours were added as <code>UiTheme.MockChipWash/MockChipInk/MockRowRule<\/code>. The driver <code>UiFidelityCodex.cs<\/code> captures the list, the 8th normal monster and the first boss at the same screen sizes with an account that has kill counts. Measured: font sizes within 0 to 0.5 px and positions within 0 to 3 px (apart from mockup sample versus real monster/skill text lengths and field tab widths). The 7 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code>. Remaining differences: logo letter-spacing width, tab height 36 px (below the 44 px touch rule, as in the mockup) and integer font-size rounding of 0.5 px. The native <code>RuntimeMonsterCodexSmoke<\/code> was adapted but not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"aspect-stone-applied-pc-1440-phone-landscape-956-phone-portrait-440-boards-and-popups\">Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)<\/h2><p>The aspect stone boards (<code>aspect<\/code> canvas, 32 boards: imprint, growth, max level, uncollected, not eligible, empty search, already imprinted, replace/new confirmation, completion, guide, gear picker, the three portrait steps) differ in structure from the old window (one card list and an imprint button below), so the whole window was redrawn in the mockup pixels. <code>AspectStoneSession<\/code> fits one canvas unit to one mockup pixel through <code>Fit<\/code> (<code>UiMock.WindowScale(safe,1440)<\/code>); the board sizes live in <code>AspectLook<\/code> (PC, landscape and portrait columns), the masthead, service bar, phone step strip and docked action bar in <code>AspectStoneWindow.Chrome.cs<\/code>, the three-panel workbench (worn gear, aspect codex, imprint preview) in <code>.Panels.cs<\/code> and <code>.Detail.cs<\/code>, the popups in <code>.Dialogs.cs<\/code>. Worn gear is ten slots (<code>aspect-slot-&lt;slot&gt;-&lt;index&gt;<\/code>) around the character figure; the codex has the search box, the class drop-down (<code>aspect-class<\/code>), four state tabs (<code>aspect-filter-0..3<\/code>: all, collected, uncollected, upgrade) and 5/4/3 columns of cards (<code>aspect-select-&lt;id&gt;<\/code>); the preview draws the five level boxes, the level-up button (<code>aspect-upgrade<\/code>), the source, the current/new aspect comparison (<code>aspect-compare<\/code>) and the notes. The imprint confirmation and its completion stay separate windows on the shared host (<code>aspect-confirm-commit/cancel<\/code>, <code>aspect-success-close<\/code>); the portrait phone uses the step strip (<code>aspect-step-1..3<\/code>) and the gear picker (<code>aspect-equipment-picker<\/code>, <code>aspect-equip-&lt;id&gt;<\/code>). Game rules (<code>AspectStone.*<\/code>, <code>UpgradeAspect<\/code>, <code>ImprintAspect<\/code>) are unchanged. The mockup&#x27;s &quot;In the field&quot; tab, demo footer and salvage demo modal are features the game does not have and are not drawn; the mockup has no close control, so <code>content-close<\/code> was added at the end of the service bar. The driver <code>UiFidelityAspect.cs<\/code> captures imprint, growth, low, max, locked, ineligible, empty, already, replace, confirm, success, help, picker, gear and detail at the same screen sizes with a nine-piece gear set and codex data (landscape uses <code>Simulate(53,27,53,8)<\/code>, portrait <code>Simulate(0,26,0,52)<\/code>). Measured: PC and portrait font sizes match exactly and positions are within 0 to 3 px (apart from row counts and font weight); landscape masthead, service bar and detail panel are within 0 to 1 px. The 48 new strings are filled in <code>en.txt<\/code> with the mockup English (the guide and part of the confirmation have no English board, so they were translated by hand). Remaining differences: the mockup&#x27;s portrait step labels collapse into two squeezed lines that overlap the number disc (a CSS bug), so they are drawn as one centred line; the mockup&#x27;s narrow landscape English folds the &quot;Equipped gear&quot; header onto two lines and pushes everything below by 17 px, which is not reproduced; the runestone SVG is replaced by the game&#x27;s existing rune art. The native <code>RuntimeAspectStoneSmoke<\/code> was adapted but not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"character-selection-screen-numeric-alignment-only-no-capture-measurement\">Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)<\/h2><p>Character creation and selection (<code>Docs/Design/CharacterPlaza<\/code>, 28 boards) is a full-screen scene with painted heroes, not a content window. <code>CharacterSelectionView<\/code> is already built from the mockup&#x27;s <code>layout()<\/code> numbers (design unit = mockup px through <code>PixelScale<\/code>), so the back button, heading, play button, crests, name tag and badge match. Reading the code against <code>layout()<\/code> line by line, only the differing values were changed: the hint pill (landscape width 320 and height 22, PC height 22, portrait 26, landscape type 12.5), the landscape badge right limit (W−112), the play type size (landscape 22), and the gear popup (panel top 150/90/58, grid side 18/21/22, gap 12/14/12, top 96/100/84, cell height 118/150/112, icon 52/60/48, name and slot type sizes, title and hero line positions, close position, a vertically centred stack inside each cell). The hero placement and animation on the scene were not touched. The screen only exists inside a signed-in <code>GameUI<\/code> session, so no editor capture driver was written, and <code>RuntimeCharacterSelectionSmoke<\/code> was not run; the visual check of this screen is still open.<\/p><h2 id=\"rune-board-measurement-baseline-only-not-redrawn\">Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)<\/h2><p>The rune board boards (<code>runeboard<\/code> canvas, 32 boards) are a new visual system: a light hex board with coloured blocks, six weapon cards, a mastery strip, storage and selection panels, and the practice, guide, preset and region dialogs, while the game&#x27;s <code>RuneBoardWindow<\/code> uses the dark stone board art and <code>U<\/code>-unit layout. <code>UiFidelityRuneBoard.cs<\/code> captured the PC, landscape and portrait baselines: its type is 1.3 to 1.4 times the mockup&#x27;s and the board art itself differs, so this is not a handful of numbers but a separate job that rebuilds the board drawing (<code>RuneBoardGraphic<\/code>) and every dialog. It risks the rune editing, tutorial and practice flows, so it was not redrawn in this pass.<\/p><h2 id=\"not-audited-yet\">Not audited yet<\/h2><p>Battle HUD graphs and observation menu (part of the whole battle HUD), redrawing the rune board (see the baseline above), and a visual check of the character selection screen. Download each canvas from Claude Design (procedure in <code>tools/ui_fidelity/README.md<\/code>) and measure it the same way.<\/p><h2 id=\"verification\">Verification<\/h2><p>Shared HUD, plaza and battle HUD: focused EditMode 112 tests (GlobalHudTests, CasualHudSharedTests, SharedUiTests, TownPlazaLayoutTests, TownWalkTests, CasualBattleHudTests, GlobalHudResourceTests, HudInspectionCasualTests) passed, daily quests and attendance: focused EditMode (DailyQuestSkinTests, AttendanceArtTests, AttendanceTests, LocalizationTests: 66 tests) passed, shared notices: 16 tests (CasualNoticeLayoutTests), town plaza layout: 19 tests (TownPlazaLayoutTests), rune master: focused EditMode 8 tests (RuneMasterCasualSkinTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed. Warehouse: focused EditMode 89 tests (StorageCasualSkinTests, StorageTests, UiButtonTests, EquipmentStorageTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed. Rift entry: focused EditMode 66 tests (RiftEntryCasualWindowTests, RiftEntryTests, LocalizationTests, CasualImportedAssetTests) passed, <code>check_ui_contract.py --verify<\/code> 15 tests passed, the native smokes were adapted but not run. Full EditMode, macOS player, devices and performance were not run.<\/p><h2 id=\"next\">Next<\/h2><p>Audit the windows under &quot;Not audited yet&quot; the same way (each needs an Editor driver <code>UiFidelity*.cs<\/code>; windows tied to GameUI are driven like <code>UiFidelityPlaza<\/code>, filling the fields without a <code>GameController<\/code> run). The skill tree already has a golden check with zero position and size difference.<\/p><h2 id=\"integration-review-correction-on-2026-10-21\">Integration review correction on 2026-10-21<\/h2><p>The official review found missing special effects, sockets and ring/dual-wield replacement selection in the new shop cards. The detail panel and bulk-sale item dialog now use the shared <code>EquipmentComparisonView<\/code>, restoring the complete tooltip, replacement targets and option ranges while retaining the transaction dock. The earlier shop-card pixel measurements do not validate this shared comparison area.<\/p>", "headings": [{"id": "method", "title": "Method"}, {"id": "shared-cause-and-shared-fix", "title": "Shared cause and shared fix"}, {"id": "warehouse-applied", "title": "Warehouse (applied)"}, {"id": "rune-master-applied", "title": "Rune master (applied)"}, {"id": "shared-confirm-and-toast-applied", "title": "Shared confirm and toast (applied)"}, {"id": "town-plaza-partly-applied", "title": "Town plaza (partly applied)"}, {"id": "daily-quests-and-attendance-applied", "title": "Daily quests and attendance (applied)"}, {"id": "shared-hud-applied", "title": "Shared HUD (applied)"}, {"id": "hunt-edict-window-legacy-partly-applied", "title": "Hunt Edict window (legacy, partly applied)"}, {"id": "settings-window-applied", "title": "Settings window (applied)"}, {"id": "equipment-shop-applied-pc-board-1440x810", "title": "Equipment shop (applied, PC board 1440x810)"}, {"id": "blacksmith-applied-pc-board-1440x810", "title": "Blacksmith (applied, PC board 1440x810)"}, {"id": "jeweler-applied-pc-and-phone-boards", "title": "Jeweler (applied, PC and phone boards)"}, {"id": "rift-entry-applied-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards-plus-eight-modals", "title": "Rift entry (applied, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards plus eight modals)"}, {"id": "rift-gear-offer-applied-portrait-440x956-and-landscape-956x440-boards", "title": "Rift gear offer (applied, portrait 440x956 and landscape 956x440 boards)"}, {"id": "training-ground-applied-lobby-add-enemy-dialog-and-three-result-states-pc-landscape-phone-and-portrait-phone-boards", "title": "Training ground (applied, lobby, add-enemy dialog and three result states, PC, landscape-phone and portrait-phone boards)"}, {"id": "monster-codex-applied-list-normal-detail-and-boss-detail-landscape-and-portrait-boards", "title": "Monster codex (applied, list, normal detail and boss detail, landscape and portrait boards)"}, {"id": "aspect-stone-applied-pc-1440-phone-landscape-956-phone-portrait-440-boards-and-popups", "title": "Aspect stone (applied, PC 1440, phone landscape 956, phone portrait 440 boards and popups)"}, {"id": "character-selection-screen-numeric-alignment-only-no-capture-measurement", "title": "Character selection screen (numeric alignment only, no capture measurement)"}, {"id": "rune-board-measurement-baseline-only-not-redrawn", "title": "Rune board (measurement baseline only, not redrawn)"}, {"id": "not-audited-yet", "title": "Not audited yet"}, {"id": "verification", "title": "Verification"}, {"id": "next", "title": "Next"}, {"id": "integration-review-correction-on-2026-10-21", "title": "Integration review correction on 2026-10-21"}]}];
